
export const govPrompts = [

    // --- POLICY WRITING & ANALYSIS (1-15) ---
    {"id":"gov-1","title":"Policy Brief for Local Government","prompt":"You are a senior policy analyst at a municipal government think tank. Write a 1,200-word policy brief for the mayor and city council of a mid-size city of 280,000 residents recommending a congestion pricing pilot program for the downtown core. Structure the brief as follows: Executive Summary (150 words, written to stand alone and be forwarded to media), Problem Definition (downtown average commute speed has fallen from 18 mph to 11 mph over five years, costing the local economy an estimated $140 million annually in lost productivity), Policy Options (three alternatives with pros and cons: Option A — variable downtown congestion charge of $5–12, Option B — expanded park-and-ride network with free transit connections, Option C — employer-based trip-reduction mandates), Recommended Option with full rationale citing evidence from Stockholm and London, Implementation Considerations covering technology procurement, equity exemptions for residents below 80% AMI, phased rollout timeline, and an independent review trigger at 18 months, Stakeholder Analysis mapping business district opposition, transit advocates, and suburban commuter concerns, and Evaluation Metrics. Tone: neutral, evidence-based, politically aware but not partisan.","tags":["policy","government","planning","transport"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:00:00Z","featured":true},

    {"id":"gov-2","title":"Regulatory Impact Assessment","prompt":"You are a senior regulator preparing a formal Regulatory Impact Assessment (RIA) for a proposed rule requiring all new residential buildings above six storeys to install grey-water recycling systems. Write the full RIA following standard OECD methodology. Include: (1) Problem statement — current per-capita water consumption, projected scarcity under climate scenarios, and market failure justification; (2) Policy objectives stated as measurable outcomes; (3) Identification of at least four alternative regulatory instruments (command-and-control, economic incentive, co-regulation, information-based) with a comparative scoring matrix against criteria of effectiveness, economic efficiency, equity, and administrative feasibility; (4) Quantified cost-benefit analysis over a 20-year horizon, including compliance costs to developers, avoided infrastructure investment, and ecosystem service values; (5) Distributional analysis identifying which groups bear the costs and which receive benefits; (6) Enforcement and compliance strategy; (7) Monitoring and evaluation framework with specific KPIs. Use plain, precise government English.","tags":["regulation","policy","analysis","environment"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:05:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-3","title":"Cabinet Decision Memo","prompt":"You are the Chief of Staff to a national minister. Draft a one-page Cabinet Decision Memo (maximum 600 words) recommending a course of action on the following issue: the national rail operator has requested emergency government funding of $800 million to avert insolvency, citing pandemic-era ridership losses that have not fully recovered. The memo must follow the standard format used in Westminster-system governments: Issue (one paragraph defining the decision required), Background (two paragraphs of essential context only — no history lesson), Options Analysis (three options laid out in a table: Option 1 full bailout with conditions, Option 2 partial bridge loan with restructuring requirements, Option 3 managed wind-down and service privatization), Recommendation with three supporting reasons, Risks and Mitigations, Consultation (which ministers have been briefed and whether there is consensus), Financial Implications, and Proposed Next Steps. Write in formal, direct British-style civil service English. Every sentence must earn its place.","tags":["cabinet","policy","decision-making","government"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:10:00Z","featured":true},

    {"id":"gov-4","title":"Evidence Review for Policy Development","prompt":"You are a government policy researcher. Conduct a structured evidence review to inform policy development on reducing youth unemployment (ages 16–24). Your review should: (1) Define the scope and research questions clearly; (2) Summarise findings from at least six categories of evidence — labour market data, international programme evaluations, cost-effectiveness studies, employer demand analysis, barriers faced by specific sub-groups (NEET youth, care leavers, young people with disabilities), and implementation lessons from previous national programmes; (3) Assess the quality of evidence for each category using a traffic-light rating (strong, moderate, limited); (4) Identify three to five policy interventions with the strongest evidence base, describing the mechanism of change, estimated effect size, and transferability caveats; (5) Flag key evidence gaps that would require primary research before major investment decisions; (6) Present a plain-language summary of what the evidence does and does not support. Do not advocate for a specific policy — present findings neutrally.","tags":["research","policy","youth","employment"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:15:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-5","title":"Sunset Clause Review Report","prompt":"You are a legislative reviewer tasked with conducting a formal sunset review of the Emergency Powers (Economic Stabilisation) Act, which was passed five years ago during a financial crisis and granted the executive branch broad authority to direct credit, fix prices, and suspend certain procurement rules. The Act contains a sunset clause requiring parliamentary renewal by the end of this calendar year. Write a 900-word sunset review report that: assesses whether the original problem conditions still exist; evaluates whether each of the Act's three main powers has been used proportionately and with adequate parliamentary oversight; presents stakeholder views (business groups, civil liberties organisations, the central bank, and opposition parties); benchmarks against equivalent emergency legislation in three comparable democracies; and concludes with a clear recommendation among three options — full renewal, renewal with restrictions, or allowed to lapse — supported by reasoning. Write in formal legislative review style.","tags":["legislation","review","accountability","governance"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:20:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-6","title":"Policy Options Paper with Stakeholder Mapping","prompt":"[Describe the policy area, the jurisdiction (national/state/local), the core problem to be solved, and the key stakeholder groups involved.]\n\nUsing the context above, produce a structured Policy Options Paper. The paper should: open with a concise problem statement (one paragraph) that quantifies the scale of the issue; map all relevant stakeholders in a table showing each group's interests, likely position (supportive/neutral/opposed), level of influence, and the primary concern that would need to be addressed to shift their position; develop three distinct policy options ranging from minimal intervention to transformative change, with each option described across six dimensions — description, mechanism, expected outcomes, estimated cost, risks, and precedents from other jurisdictions; provide a multi-criteria analysis matrix scoring each option against effectiveness, cost, equity, political feasibility, speed of impact, and administrative complexity; conclude with a preferred option recommendation and a 90-day action plan for the first implementation steps. Target length: 1,000–1,200 words.","tags":["policy","stakeholders","analysis","government"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:25:00Z","featured":true},

    {"id":"gov-7","title":"Green Paper Consultation Document","prompt":"You are a policy director in a national ministry preparing a Green Paper (consultation document) on reforming the national social housing system. Draft a 1,000-word consultation document that: opens with a ministerial foreword explaining why reform is needed now; presents the current state of social housing using a structured diagnosis (stock levels, waiting list data, condition surveys, funding trends over 10 years); sets out the government's reform objectives using SMART criteria; poses eight specific consultation questions — four closed (yes/no with explanation space) and four open — that will elicit genuine stakeholder input rather than validate a predetermined conclusion; explains the consultation process including how responses will be analysed and fed into final policy; and closes with a clear statement of what the government has already decided versus what remains genuinely open for consultation. Avoid bureaucratic padding. Every question must be answerable by a range of respondents including tenants, local authorities, housing associations, and developers.","tags":["consultation","housing","policy","engagement"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:30:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-8","title":"Post-Implementation Review","prompt":"You are a programme evaluator writing a Post-Implementation Review (PIR) of a digital government initiative launched three years ago: a mandatory online platform for small business permit applications that replaced a paper-based system. The original business case projected £12 million in annual savings, a 40% reduction in processing time, and a 90% user satisfaction score. Write the PIR following HM Treasury guidance. Include: methodology (data sources, evaluation design, limitations); findings against each of the three projected outcomes using actual data (processing time reduced by 22%, satisfaction score 67%, savings £6.8 million); analysis of what drove the gaps between projected and actual performance; identification of unintended consequences — both negative (digital exclusion of 18% of small businesses without internet access) and positive (unexpected reduction in incomplete applications); lessons learned categorised for policy, delivery, and digital design teams; and recommendations for the next phase of the programme. Write in evaluation report style — objective, evidence-based, and candid about failure.","tags":["evaluation","digital","policy","audit"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:35:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-9","title":"Interministerial Policy Coordination Framework","prompt":"You are a senior official in the Cabinet Office tasked with improving coordination between five ministries — Health, Education, Housing, Finance, and Social Services — on a shared policy agenda to reduce child poverty by 20% over five years. Design a detailed interministerial coordination framework. Include: governance structure (who chairs, voting or consensus, escalation path to Cabinet); terms of reference for a joint working group; a shared outcomes framework with lead and contributing ministry responsibilities for each indicator; a protocol for resolving policy conflicts when ministries have competing budget or legislative priorities; a joint data-sharing agreement outline covering what data each ministry contributes, under what legal basis, and with what privacy safeguards; a quarterly reporting rhythm with standardised reporting templates; and a communications protocol for announcing joint initiatives. The framework should be practical enough to operate without constant ministerial attention, but with clear escalation triggers when political decisions are needed.","tags":["coordination","governance","interministerial","policy"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:40:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-10","title":"Comparative Policy Analysis","prompt":"[Name the policy issue you want to analyse, and specify two or three countries or jurisdictions whose approaches you want to compare.]\n\nConduct a rigorous comparative policy analysis on the topic and jurisdictions identified above. Your analysis should: provide a clear tertium comparationis — the common analytical framework applied equally to all jurisdictions; describe each jurisdiction's approach covering problem framing, policy instruments used, institutional arrangements, funding mechanisms, and how the policy has evolved over time; compile a comparative data table covering key outcome indicators (use publicly available data and note data vintage); identify the three most important contextual factors (political system, demographic structure, administrative capacity, cultural norms, etc.) that explain why a policy that works in one jurisdiction may not transfer directly; highlight one genuine innovation from each jurisdiction that the commissioning government could adapt; and conclude with three transferable lessons and two significant adaptation challenges. Use neutral analytical language — this is evidence synthesis, not advocacy.","tags":["comparative","policy","international","analysis"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:45:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-11","title":"Annual Departmental Review","prompt":"You are a director-general preparing the annual performance review of a large government department (Ministry of Infrastructure, 4,200 staff, annual budget $2.1 billion). Write a structured 800-word internal performance narrative covering: delivery against the five strategic objectives set at the start of the year (use illustrative but realistic targets and results, including one area of strong overperformance, two areas meeting expectations, one area with modest shortfall, and one area significantly below target); a candid analysis of the reasons for the shortfall area — not excuses, but root cause analysis; financial stewardship covering budget variance, underspend reallocation, and any audit findings; workforce summary covering headcount, diversity progress, sick leave trends, and a staff engagement score; key risks materialised during the year and how they were managed; and priorities and resource asks for the year ahead. Write in the candid, self-critical tone that distinguishes a credible internal review from a public relations exercise.","tags":["performance","reporting","management","government"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:50:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-12","title":"Cost-Benefit Analysis for a Public Programme","prompt":"[Describe the public programme or project to be analysed, including its main activities, target population, projected scale, and any available data on costs or outcomes.]\n\nConduct a rigorous cost-benefit analysis (CBA) for the programme described above, following HM Treasury Green Book methodology (or equivalent). The analysis must: define the counterfactual clearly; itemise all relevant costs (capital, operating, opportunity costs, and transitional costs) over a ten-year appraisal period with appropriate discounting; identify and, where possible, monetise all relevant benefits — direct programme outcomes, wider economic impacts, and non-market values using established methods (SROI, willingness to pay, avoided costs); present results as Net Present Value, Benefit-Cost Ratio, and Internal Rate of Return; conduct a sensitivity analysis varying the three most uncertain parameters by ±25% and show how results change; discuss distributional impacts — who bears costs and who receives benefits, disaggregated by income quintile and geography where data allow; present a plain-English summary table for decision-makers who will not read the technical annex; and conclude with an honest assessment of the analysis's limitations.","tags":["finance","economics","evaluation","policy"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T10:55:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-13","title":"Policy Failure Post-Mortem","prompt":"You are an independent reviewer commissioned to write a post-mortem on a significant policy failure: a national school meals programme launched two years ago that was scrapped after 14 months due to cost overruns (actual cost 2.3x the estimate), delivery failures (only 61% of eligible children enrolled by month 12), and a damaging parliamentary inquiry. Write a 1,000-word structured post-mortem that: objectively describes what happened and the sequence of decisions; identifies the root causes categorised across four domains — policy design flaws, implementation failures, governance weaknesses, and external factors; assesses what warning signs were present but not acted upon; names the systemic failure patterns (optimism bias, insufficient piloting, fragmented accountability, poor data systems) without scapegoating individuals; and produces 8 concrete recommendations — four for improving future policy design and four for improving implementation governance. Write in the tone of a candid professional review, not a political attack.","tags":["evaluation","accountability","policy","learning"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T11:00:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-14","title":"National Strategy Document","prompt":"[Provide the strategic theme (e.g. digital inclusion, food security, ageing population), the jurisdiction level, any known political constraints, and the time horizon for the strategy.]\n\nDraft the core narrative sections of a national strategy document on the topic provided. The strategy document should include: an opening vision statement (aspirational but credibly grounded — avoid empty rhetoric); a situational analysis covering current state, trends, and projected future state without intervention; a clear statement of strategic priorities — no more than five, each expressed as an outcome rather than an activity; for each priority, a logic model showing the theory of change, key levers available to government, and indicators of success; a whole-of-government delivery map showing which ministry leads, which ministries contribute, and what the non-government actors (private sector, civil society, local government) must do; a resource mobilisation section identifying existing budget, required new investment, and financing mechanisms; a risk register covering the top five risks to strategy delivery with mitigation approaches; and a monitoring and adaptation framework specifying annual review points and conditions that would trigger a strategy revision. Write at a level appropriate for public release.","tags":["strategy","national","planning","government"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T11:05:00Z","featured":true},

    {"id":"gov-15","title":"Regulatory Sandbox Design","prompt":"You are a senior regulator in a financial services authority designing a regulatory sandbox to allow fintech startups to test innovative products (cryptocurrency savings accounts, AI-based credit scoring, embedded insurance) under controlled conditions without full regulatory compliance. Write a detailed sandbox design document covering: eligibility criteria and the application assessment process; the specific regulatory requirements that will be waived or modified during the sandbox period, with the legal basis for each waiver; consumer protection safeguards that remain non-negotiable during testing; the data and reporting requirements placed on participants to allow the regulator to monitor for harm; the maximum scale limits (number of customers, transaction volume, geographic scope) for sandbox participants; the exit process covering how successful innovations graduate to full authorisation and how failed tests are wound down safely; governance of the sandbox including who makes admission decisions, how conflicts of interest are managed, and parliamentary reporting; and a lessons-learned process ensuring insights from the sandbox feed into permanent regulatory reform. Draw on the UK FCA, Singapore MAS, and Australian ASIC sandbox models.","tags":["regulation","fintech","innovation","sandbox"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-15T11:10:00Z","featured":false},

    // --- LEGISLATIVE DRAFTING (16-25) ---
    {"id":"gov-16","title":"Municipal Ordinance Drafting","prompt":"[Describe the behaviour or activity the ordinance is intended to regulate, the municipality's jurisdiction, any existing related bylaws, and the enforcement mechanism available to the municipality.]\n\nDraft a complete municipal ordinance on the subject described above. The ordinance must follow proper legislative drafting conventions and include: a preamble citing statutory authority and stating the public interest served; a definitions section that precisely defines every term used in operative clauses; operative sections with numbered subsections establishing clear obligations, prohibitions, permissions, and standards; an exceptions and exemptions section; a penalty and enforcement section specifying infraction categories, fine schedules, and the right of appeal; an administration section identifying the responsible officer and their powers of inspection, notice, and order; a transition provision for existing operators or structures; a severability clause; and an effective date. Avoid ambiguity in every operative clause — each clause should be capable of only one reasonable interpretation. Note any provisions that would need legal review before enactment.","tags":["legislation","ordinance","drafting","local-government"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-16T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-17","title":"Model Legislation: Data Privacy in Public Services","prompt":"Draft model legislation for a national Data Privacy in Public Services Act. The legislation should govern how government departments, agencies, and publicly funded service providers collect, use, store, share, and delete personal data. Include: legislative purpose and scope (covering which bodies are regulated and which data types); a citizens' rights chapter (right of access, rectification, erasure, portability, and objection to automated decision-making); a government obligations chapter (data minimisation, purpose limitation, storage limitation, security standards, privacy by design, data impact assessments); special category data provisions for health, biometric, religious, and political data; rules on data sharing between government bodies and with third parties; an independent supervisory authority with defined powers of investigation, enforcement, and fining; sanctions graduated by severity and organisational size; a judicial review pathway for individuals; and a transitional chapter for legacy data systems. Draft in the style of an enabling Act with detail relegated to secondary regulations where appropriate.","tags":["legislation","data-privacy","digital","rights"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-16T10:05:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-18","title":"Legislative Drafting Instructions","prompt":"You are a senior legislative counsel. A policy team has provided the following policy intent: the government wants to require large employers (500+ employees) to publish annual gender pay gap reports, include a statement of action, and face escalating fines for non-compliance after two years. Translate this policy intent into formal legislative drafting instructions (sometimes called instructions to Parliamentary Counsel) covering: definitions of key terms including 'employer,' 'pay gap,' and 'relevant pay data'; the precise calculation methodology to be mandated; the publication format, platform, and deadline; the content requirements for the statement of action; the enforcement body and its investigatory powers; the penalty structure including first-year warning, second-year fixed penalty, and third-year escalating daily fines; whistleblower protections for employees who provide information to the enforcement body; and any necessary Henry VIII powers for ministers to update calculation methodologies by secondary legislation. Flag three legal risks the Parliamentary Counsel should be aware of.","tags":["legislation","drafting","equality","employment"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-16T10:10:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-19","title":"Plain Language Translation of Complex Law","prompt":"The following is an extract from a national tax code dealing with transfer pricing rules for multinational enterprises. It is written in dense legal and technical language that most business owners and taxpayers cannot understand. Your task is to rewrite this content as a plain language guide for a small-to-medium business owner who has no legal or tax training. The guide must: explain what the rule applies to and who is affected; describe in simple terms what behaviour is required (not just prohibited); give two worked examples — one showing compliant practice, one showing non-compliant practice — using realistic numbers; explain the consequences of non-compliance in plain terms; answer the five most likely questions a reader would have after reading the plain text; and provide a glossary of the five most important technical terms used. Standards: all sentences under 25 words where possible; no nominalisations (e.g. write 'we require' not 'there is a requirement'); active voice throughout; reading level should be accessible to someone with a secondary school education. [Paste the legislative text to be translated below this line.]","tags":["plain-language","legislation","accessibility","tax"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-16T10:15:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-20","title":"Amending Bill: Gap Analysis","prompt":"You are a parliamentary drafter. The existing Residential Tenancies Act has three documented gaps that a new amending bill must close: (1) landlords can currently increase rent with only 14 days' notice, leaving tenants insufficient time to budget or seek alternative housing; (2) the Act does not cover informal sub-letting arrangements, leaving sub-tenants with no legal protections; (3) there is no mechanism for tenants to withhold rent when landlords fail to make urgent structural repairs within a defined timeframe. For each gap: draft the specific amending clause in proper legislative style; explain the legal technique used (amendment, insertion, substitution, repeal); identify any consequential amendments required to other sections of the Act; flag any constitutional or human rights considerations; and note any transitional provisions needed for existing tenancies. Present the full set of amendments as they would appear in a Schedule to an Amending Bill, including the Bill's long title.","tags":["legislation","amendments","tenancy","housing"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-16T10:20:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-21","title":"Secondary Legislation: Statutory Instrument","prompt":"The Public Health (Communicable Diseases) Act 2020 grants the Minister of Health power to make regulations specifying which diseases are notifiable, the timeframe for notification, who must report, and to whom. Draft the Statutory Instrument (Notifiable Diseases Regulations 2025) to exercise these powers. The SI should include: citation and commencement; interpretation section; a Schedule of notifiable diseases (include at least 15 realistic diseases with columns for the required notification period — immediate, within 24 hours, within 3 days — and whether laboratory confirmation is required or clinical diagnosis is sufficient); obligations on medical practitioners, laboratories, and hospital administrators with specific timeframes for each; the form of notification (electronic portal with specified data fields); confidentiality protections for patient data; an amendment procedure; and penalties for failure to notify. Ensure the instrument stays strictly within the scope of the enabling power.","tags":["legislation","secondary","public-health","drafting"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-16T10:25:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-22","title":"Treaty Implementation Legislation","prompt":"Country A has signed a bilateral investment treaty (BIT) with Country B that requires domestic legislation to be fully effective. The BIT grants investors from each country national treatment, most-favoured-nation treatment, protection against expropriation without compensation, free transfer of funds, and access to investor-state dispute settlement (ISDS) through international arbitration. Draft the outline of an Investment Protection (Country B) Act that gives effect to these obligations in domestic law. Include: purpose and interpretation section; national treatment provisions and how they interact with existing sector-specific restrictions; the compensation standard for expropriation (specifying Hull formula or alternative); the fund transfer provisions and permitted exceptions (prudential measures, taxation, court orders); the arbitration gateway provisions selecting ICSID and specifying the cooling-off period; the domestic court relationship with arbitral awards including enforcement and limited grounds for challenge; and any parliamentary oversight mechanism for ISDS awards. Note three areas where the legislature has discretion in implementing these BIT obligations and explain the policy trade-offs.","tags":["international","legislation","investment","treaties"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-16T10:30:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-23","title":"Legislative Scrutiny Memorandum","prompt":"You are a parliamentary committee analyst. A government bill — the Critical Infrastructure Protection Bill — has been introduced into parliament. The bill grants a minister power to designate any digital system as 'critical infrastructure' by written notice, impose security requirements on designated operators with fines of up to $10 million for non-compliance, and authorise government access to operator systems for 'monitoring purposes' without a warrant. Write a legislative scrutiny memorandum for the committee examining the bill. The memorandum should: summarise the bill's main provisions; flag each provision that raises human rights, rule of law, or parliamentary sovereignty concerns and explain why; assess whether the ministerial powers are sufficiently defined or constitute unacceptably broad Henry VIII clauses; evaluate the adequacy of the safeguards, judicial oversight, and parliamentary oversight mechanisms; compare the bill's approach to equivalent legislation in three comparable democracies; and propose 10 specific amendments the committee should seek, each with a brief rationale. Write in the objective, analytical style of a professional parliamentary researcher.","tags":["parliament","scrutiny","legislation","rights"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-16T10:35:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-24","title":"Delegated Legislation Review","prompt":"You are a member of a Delegated Powers and Regulatory Reform Committee. Review the following scenario: a new Digital Economy Bill contains 47 separate delegated powers — clauses that allow ministers or regulators to make, amend, or repeal rules without primary legislation. Write a structured review report that: explains the constitutional significance of delegated powers and the principles used to assess them (breadth of power, procedural controls, justification, and sunset provisions); selects five of the most significant delegated powers and applies the assessment framework to each; identifies which powers should be subject to the affirmative resolution procedure (requiring active parliamentary approval) rather than the negative procedure (taking effect unless parliament objects); recommends three powers that are so broad they should be removed from the bill and replaced with primary legislation provisions; and produces a model clause showing what an appropriately drafted, limited delegated power would look like in this context. Write for a parliamentary audience.","tags":["parliament","delegated-legislation","scrutiny","governance"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-16T10:40:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-25","title":"Bill Committee Stage Amendments","prompt":"[Describe the bill being examined, the policy gap or rights concern you want to address, and whether you are acting as a government minister seeking to improve the bill or as an opposition member seeking to strengthen protections.]\n\nDraft three substantive amendments to the bill described above for tabling at Committee Stage. For each amendment: draft the precise amendment text in correct parliamentary drafting format (specifying the clause, line, and the words to be inserted, deleted, or substituted); write a brief explanatory note (100 words) explaining what the amendment achieves and why it is needed; prepare a 3-minute speaking note for the MP or minister moving the amendment, covering the policy argument, addressing the most likely government objection, and citing one relevant precedent; and identify any consequential amendments to other parts of the bill that your amendment would require. Ensure each amendment is technically sound — it should be capable of achieving its stated purpose without creating unintended consequences.","tags":["parliament","amendments","legislation","drafting"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-16T10:45:00Z","featured":false},

    // --- GOVERNMENT COMMUNICATIONS (26-37) ---
    {"id":"gov-26","title":"Ministerial Statement to Parliament","prompt":"You are a senior speech writer in a minister's office. The Minister for Transport must make an oral statement to parliament following a major failure: a new national rail booking system, procured at a cost of £340 million and delayed twice, has gone live and crashed on day one, stranding 180,000 passengers and generating 48 hours of negative media coverage. Write the full ministerial statement (approximately 700 words, 8–10 minutes speaking time). The statement must: open by taking full ministerial responsibility without deflecting blame; give a factual account of what happened and the current situation; confirm the immediate remediation actions taken and the timeline for restoring full service; announce a specific independent review with defined terms and a reporting date; address the procurement process and whether the contract contains performance penalties; make specific commitments to passengers affected (refunds, compensation); and pre-empt the three strongest opposition questions with brief acknowledgements. Tone: accountable, calm, focused on solutions. Avoid defensive language or blame-shifting.","tags":["parliament","communications","crisis","minister"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-17T10:00:00Z","featured":true},

    {"id":"gov-27","title":"Government Press Release","prompt":"[Provide the policy announcement, programme launch, or statistical release that needs a press release. Include the key facts, figures, and the name and title of the minister or official to be quoted.]\n\nWrite a government press release for the announcement described above. Follow UK Government press release conventions: a headline that states the news — not a label, but a sentence with a verb; a standfirst summarising the top line in one sentence; an opening paragraph containing the most important information (what, who, when, where, why) written so that a journalist could use it as a standalone story; three to four body paragraphs developing the story with specific data, context, and additional detail; one attributed ministerial quote (2–3 sentences: the policy rationale, the benefit to citizens, and one forward-looking statement); one supporting quote from a stakeholder, partner, or beneficiary; a background notes section with factual context, links to supporting documents, and statistical methodology; and media contact details. Total length: 400–500 words excluding background notes. Tone: factual, positive but not hyperbolic, accessible to a general audience.","tags":["communications","pr","media","government"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-17T10:05:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-28","title":"Crisis Communications Plan for Government","prompt":"You are the Director of Communications for a large city. At 6:47 AM on a Tuesday, a major water main rupture has contaminated the drinking water supply for 340,000 residents in four boroughs. The contamination has been confirmed as bacterial. Residents should boil water before use. The situation is expected to last 72–96 hours. Create a detailed crisis communications plan for the first 72 hours. Include: an immediate response checklist for the first 60 minutes (what to post, who to notify, which channels to activate); holding statement and first public alert message (suitable for SMS broadcast, website, and social media simultaneously); a communications rhythm — specifying what type of update goes out at 2-hour, 6-hour, and 24-hour intervals, and who authorises each; channel strategy covering Twitter/X, Facebook, local radio, WhatsApp community groups, digital signage, and door-to-door teams for areas with low digital access; key messages for four distinct audiences (families with young children, elderly residents, businesses, and healthcare facilities); a rumour management protocol; and a 72-hour media briefing schedule including a template press conference script. Include a checklist for transitioning from crisis to recovery communications.","tags":["crisis","communications","emergency","government"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-17T10:10:00Z","featured":true},

    {"id":"gov-29","title":"Annual Report Narrative","prompt":"You are a communications director drafting the narrative sections of a government department's Annual Report. The Department for Education and Skills has had a mixed year: GCSE attainment at grade 4+ rose 2.3 percentage points to 68.4%, but the gap between disadvantaged pupils and their peers widened slightly; 12,400 new teachers were recruited against a target of 15,000; 94 new schools opened; the special educational needs (SEND) reform programme is two years behind schedule; and staff engagement within the department fell to 58% from 64% the previous year. Write: an Accounting Officer's foreword (400 words) that is candid about both progress and shortfalls and sets the tone for accountability; a performance summary section (600 words) covering the four main outcome areas using the data above; an analysis of how the department managed its three highest-rated risks during the year; and a 'lessons learned' section (200 words) identifying two areas where the department will do things differently. Write in the honest, balanced tone required by public accountability standards — not a promotional document.","tags":["annual-report","communications","accountability","performance"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-17T10:15:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-30","title":"Public Health Campaign Script","prompt":"You are a senior communications strategist working for a national public health agency. Design and write a complete digital communications campaign to increase uptake of flu vaccination among adults aged 50–64, a group where coverage has stalled at 52% for three years. The campaign deliverables should include: a campaign strategy brief covering target audience insight (barriers to vaccination based on research evidence: inconvenience, vaccine hesitancy, perceived low personal risk), campaign objectives (specific, measurable), core message platform and tone of voice; a 30-second radio script; a set of four social media posts — one for Facebook (text-dominant), one for Instagram (image-led with minimal text), one for Twitter/X (under 280 characters), and one for WhatsApp (conversational); a 200-word email to patients from their GP surgery; and a countering-misinformation messaging guide with suggested responses to the five most common vaccine-hesitancy claims. All content must be inclusive of people with low health literacy. Do not use fear-based messaging.","tags":["public-health","communications","campaign","health"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-17T10:20:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-31","title":"FOI Response Drafting","prompt":"You are a government information officer. A Freedom of Information request has been received asking for all internal communications between senior officials and the lobby group 'Infrastructure First' in the 18 months preceding the award of a major road construction contract. A preliminary review has identified 47 relevant documents: 32 can be disclosed in full, 9 must be redacted under the commercial interests exemption (Section 43 of the FOIA), 4 must be redacted under the policy advice exemption (Section 35), and 2 cannot be released at all under national security grounds (Section 24). The public interest test applies to all three exemptions. Write: a full response letter to the requester explaining what is being released, what is being withheld, and the specific legal basis and public interest reasoning for each category of withholding; a summary schedule of the 47 documents; the standard refusal language for each exemption category; information on the requester's rights to request an internal review and to complain to the Information Commissioner; and a note for the file documenting the public interest balance test for each exemption applied.","tags":["FOI","transparency","legal","government"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-17T10:25:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-32","title":"Citizen Letter: Complex Decision Explanation","prompt":"[Describe the government decision or determination that needs to be communicated, the citizen's situation, the decision outcome, and the reason for it.]\n\nWrite a letter from a government agency to a citizen explaining the decision described above. The letter must meet the following standards: it must be readable by someone with a lower secondary education level — use short sentences, active voice, and plain vocabulary; it must state the decision clearly in the first sentence — the citizen should not have to read past paragraph one to know the outcome; it must explain the reasons in a logical sequence using the facts of the citizen's specific case, not just recite the legal provisions; it must acknowledge any aspects of the situation that are difficult for the citizen; it must clearly explain what the citizen can do next — whether that is appealing, providing further information, or accepting the decision; it must give specific contact details for further help; and it must not contain unexplained acronyms, legal jargon, or passive constructions that obscure who made the decision. Maximum length: 400 words. Attach a one-paragraph plain-English summary at the top for citizens who will read only that.","tags":["communications","citizen-services","plain-language","letters"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-17T10:30:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-33","title":"Disinformation Response Protocol","prompt":"You are the Head of Digital Communications for a national government. Your monitoring team has detected a rapidly spreading false claim on social media alleging that a new national digital identity card will allow the government to track citizens' real-time physical locations. The claim has 180,000 shares in 6 hours and is being amplified by two prominent political commentators and a foreign state-adjacent social media account. Write a complete disinformation response protocol covering: the internal escalation and decision-making process for the first 4 hours; criteria for deciding between a rapid public rebuttal versus a more measured response (and the risks of each); the rebuttal content strategy — what specific, factual information must be communicated and how to do so without inadvertently amplifying the false claim; platform-specific response tactics for Twitter/X, Facebook, YouTube, and Telegram; engagement guidance for departmental staff who encounter the claim personally; legal considerations including defamation, official corrections to broadcasters, and options for platform content reporting; and a post-incident review process to update the protocol. Avoid being dismissive of citizen privacy concerns — acknowledge them while correcting the specific false claim.","tags":["disinformation","crisis","digital","communications"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-17T10:35:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-34","title":"Multilingual Public Service Notice","prompt":"You are a communications manager for a metropolitan local authority with a highly diverse population. The council is implementing new waste collection arrangements starting next month: households will receive a new food waste bin (brown, 23 litres) collected weekly; the existing grey general waste bin will move from weekly to fortnightly collection; and a new booking system for bulky item collection replaces the previous free drop-off service. Write a comprehensive communication that: contains the core information in English using plain language at a Grade 6 reading level or below; identifies the seven languages other than English spoken by at least 2% of the borough's population (you may select plausible examples such as Punjabi, Somali, Polish, Bengali, Arabic, Mandarin, and Romanian) and provides guidance on how the council should approach professional translation for each; writes a version of the core notice at B1 English level for communities where English is a second language; specifies the non-written communication channels required to reach residents with very low literacy; and provides a checklist for testing whether the communications have been understood before the new system launches.","tags":["accessibility","communications","local-government","inclusion"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-17T10:40:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-35","title":"Ministerial Media Lines","prompt":"[Describe the policy issue or announcement, the political context, and the three most difficult questions the minister is likely to face in a media interview or press conference.]\n\nPrepare a comprehensive media briefing pack for the ministerial interview described above. The pack should include: a two-paragraph 'top line' message — what the minister most needs the public to take away from this interview; an attack-proof opening statement (60 seconds) that gets the minister's message in before the interviewer takes control; 'lines to take' for each of the three difficult questions identified — each line should include an honest acknowledgement of the concern, the government's position with supporting evidence, a pivot back to the positive message, and a 'door-closing' phrase for follow-ups; three proactive talking points the minister should try to introduce even if not asked; four phrases to avoid (and the alternative phrasing to use instead); guidance on handling a direct factual challenge from the interviewer; and a one-page background briefing note the minister should read the night before. All lines should be factually defensible and politically sustainable.","tags":["media","communications","minister","briefing"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-17T10:45:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-36","title":"Government Social Media Strategy","prompt":"You are the Head of Digital Engagement for a national ministry. The ministry currently has a minimal social media presence — a Twitter/X account with 18,000 followers and a Facebook page that is rarely updated. The minister wants to significantly increase the ministry's digital engagement to build public trust, communicate directly with citizens, and counteract disinformation about the ministry's programmes. Design a 12-month social media strategy. Cover: platform strategy — which platforms to prioritise (including an assessment of emerging platforms) and the rationale; audience segmentation and the distinct content approach for each segment; a content pillar framework (what types of content — educational, behind-the-scenes, data visualisations, citizen stories — in what proportion); a content calendar structure for one month showing cadence and variety; a community management policy specifying how to handle complaints, hate speech, requests for individual case help, and political attacks; a crisis escalation protocol for social media incidents; governance including approval chains for different content types; measurement framework with specific KPIs for reach, engagement, trust, and service deflection; and resource requirements. Note: all proposals must comply with civil service social media standards.","tags":["social-media","digital","communications","strategy"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-17T10:50:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-37","title":"Public Inquiry Witness Statement","prompt":"You are the Permanent Secretary of a government department who has been called to give evidence to a public inquiry into the government's handling of a public health emergency. The inquiry has sent you a written request to address three specific matters: (1) the timeline of your department's internal decision-making between January 15 and March 30 when key decisions about PPE procurement were made; (2) the advice you gave ministers and whether that advice was followed; (3) the lessons you have drawn from the department's performance and what changes have been made. Write a 1,200-word witness statement that: opens with your formal credentials and your role during the relevant period; addresses each of the three matters in sequence with specific dates and decisions rather than generalities; is candid about what the department got right and what it got wrong; does not attribute blame to ministers while also accurately representing the advice they received; and closes with a genuine reflection on systemic improvements. Write in the formal, precise style appropriate for a public inquiry, where every sentence may be scrutinised.","tags":["inquiry","accountability","legal","government"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-17T10:55:00Z","featured":false},

    // --- PUBLIC PROCUREMENT (38-47) ---
    {"id":"gov-38","title":"Request for Proposal: IT System","prompt":"You are a chief procurement officer. Draft the complete technical and commercial specification sections for a Request for Proposal (RFP) for a new integrated case management system for a large social services department (3,400 case workers, 190,000 active cases annually, integration required with courts, police, healthcare, and housing systems). The RFP specification must include: system architecture requirements (cloud-native, multi-tenant SaaS preferred; UK data residency mandatory; API-first for integration); functional requirements presented as a prioritised MoSCoW list covering at least 25 specific requirements; non-functional requirements covering performance benchmarks (page load, concurrent users, disaster recovery), security standards (minimum ISO 27001, Cyber Essentials Plus), accessibility (WCAG 2.1 AA), and data portability; integration requirements for each of the four external systems with data field mapping; data migration requirements from the legacy system; a detailed evaluation scoring matrix with weightings for technical capability (35%), commercial value (25%), implementation approach and risk (20%), and supplier track record (20%); contract terms provisions including service level agreements, exit rights, and data deletion on termination; and a supplier questions template for the clarification period.","tags":["procurement","IT","government","contracts"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-18T10:00:00Z","featured":true},

    {"id":"gov-39","title":"Procurement Strategy Note","prompt":"[Describe the goods or services to be procured, the estimated contract value, the procurement context, any known market conditions, and the key risks you want the procurement strategy to address.]\n\nWrite a Procurement Strategy Note (PSN) for the procurement described above, following government procurement best practice. The PSN should cover: contract scope and objectives; market analysis covering the number of capable suppliers, market concentration, supplier financial stability, and any known barriers to SME participation; an assessment of three sourcing options (open competition, restricted competition, framework agreement, or direct award) with a recommended approach and rationale; procurement route under the relevant procurement regulations with justification; commercial model options (fixed price, time-and-materials, outcome-based, shared savings) with recommended approach; key contract terms and risk allocation between buyer and supplier; evaluation methodology including criteria, weightings, and the approach to most economically advantageous tender (MEAT); timeline from OJEU/Find-a-Tender notice to contract award; risk register covering the top five procurement risks; and approval requirements and governance. Write in the format used by government commercial teams.","tags":["procurement","commercial","strategy","government"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-18T10:05:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-40","title":"Tender Evaluation Report","prompt":"You are a procurement officer. A tender evaluation panel has assessed four bids for a five-year facilities management contract worth £45 million. The evaluation results are: Supplier A scored 78/100 (technical 42/60, commercial 36/40); Supplier B scored 74/100 (technical 51/60, commercial 23/40); Supplier C scored 71/100 (technical 44/60, commercial 27/40); Supplier D scored 58/100 (technical 38/60, commercial 20/40) and was disqualified on technical grounds for failing to meet a mandatory requirement for ISO 45001 certification. Write a complete tender evaluation report that: summarises the procurement process and confirms it was conducted in accordance with the procurement regulations; documents the evaluation panel membership and the management of conflicts of interest; presents the scoring summary table and the narrative justification for each supplier's scores in the technical and commercial dimensions; explains the disqualification of Supplier D; confirms the recommended award to Supplier A with the rationale; notes the standstill obligations before contract award; and identifies any lessons learned for future procurements. Write in the precise, defensible style required for a document that may be subject to a legal challenge from unsuccessful bidders.","tags":["procurement","evaluation","contracts","government"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-18T10:10:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-41","title":"Social Value in Procurement","prompt":"You are developing a Social Value Framework for a metropolitan council's procurement programme (total annual spend: £380 million). The council wants to use its purchasing power to generate employment, skills, community benefits, and environmental improvements in the local area, going beyond the minimum requirements of the UK Social Value Act. Write a comprehensive Social Value Framework covering: the council's social value priorities aligned to its Corporate Plan (local employment, carbon reduction, mental health, and support for voluntary sector); the social value measurement model — specifying which outcomes will be measured using HACT Social Value Bank values, which will be scored qualitatively, and which will be tracked as outputs; minimum social value requirements by contract category and value threshold (supply of goods contracts below £100k, service contracts £100k–£500k, major contracts above £500k); requirements for how suppliers demonstrate social value at bid stage, agree targets at contract award, and report performance quarterly; a mechanism for rewarding genuine social value delivery through contract reviews versus penalising under-delivery; guidance for evaluators on how to assess social value claims to avoid greenwashing; and a council-wide social value target for the year with a baseline.","tags":["procurement","social-value","community","sustainability"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-18T10:15:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-42","title":"Contract Management Framework","prompt":"A government department has recently awarded a 10-year IT outsourcing contract worth $1.2 billion to a single supplier. The department has a history of poor contract management that has led to supplier cost overruns and service failures on previous contracts. Design a Contract Management Framework for this contract. The framework should cover: governance structure — the contract management team structure, reporting lines, and the escalation path from contract manager to Accounting Officer; a relationship management model with defined meetings, agenda templates, and attendance requirements at strategic, operational, and tactical levels; performance management covering the 12 contractual KPIs, measurement methodology, reporting formats, and the consequence management process for underperformance (service credits, improvement plans, termination triggers); financial management covering invoice approval, benchmarking rights, open-book accounting provisions, and contract variation management; risk management with a register of the top 10 contract risks and the monitoring regime; supplier dependency management and exit planning requirements starting from year three; a benefits realisation tracker aligned to the original business case; and annual contract review process including independent audit rights.","tags":["procurement","contract-management","IT","governance"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-18T10:20:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-43","title":"Procurement Fraud Prevention Policy","prompt":"You are the Chief Procurement Officer. Recent internal audit findings have identified three procurement fraud risks: (1) split purchasing — staff are dividing orders to avoid competitive tender thresholds; (2) fraudulent suppliers — two vendor records in the system share bank account details with employee records; (3) conflicts of interest — three procurement officers did not declare relationships with suppliers during recent evaluations. Write a comprehensive Procurement Fraud Prevention Policy covering: the policy statement and the roles and responsibilities of all staff involved in procurement at any level; a complete set of internal controls targeting split purchasing (including system-level purchase order controls and management exception reports), supplier due diligence (including bank account verification protocols and beneficial ownership checks), and conflict of interest management (mandatory declaration requirements, recusal procedures, and post-employment cooling-off rules); a detection regime covering data analytics, internal audit sampling, and whistleblowing mechanisms; the investigation process when fraud is suspected; disciplinary and criminal referral procedures; training requirements; and a policy effectiveness review cycle.","tags":["procurement","fraud","compliance","governance"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-18T10:25:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-44","title":"Market Engagement Strategy","prompt":"A national health agency plans to procure a new national patient record system — a complex, high-value digital programme ($600 million over 15 years) with no direct precedent in the domestic market. Before formally launching the procurement, the agency wants to conduct meaningful market engagement to test appetite, shape requirements, and promote competition. Design a pre-market engagement strategy covering: the objectives of market engagement (what the agency needs to learn from the market); the engagement methods to be used (Request for Information, supplier briefings, one-to-one meetings, industry days, alpha prototyping) and the sequencing and timeline; the information the agency will share with the market and the safeguards for commercial confidentiality; the approach to managing the risk of inadvertently advantaging one supplier through market engagement; how engagement with large incumbent suppliers will be managed alongside engagement with SMEs and new entrants; the legal framework governing pre-market engagement under the applicable procurement regulations; how the insights from market engagement will be used to finalise requirements, procurement strategy, and commercial model; and the documentation required to demonstrate the engagement was fair and transparent.","tags":["procurement","market-engagement","digital","health"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-18T10:30:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-45","title":"SME Procurement Action Plan","prompt":"Analysis of a central government department's procurement data shows that only 8% of its contract spend reaches small and medium-sized enterprises (SMEs) directly, compared to a government target of 33%. The bulk of spend is concentrated in five large prime contractors. Write an SME Procurement Action Plan to close this gap over three years. The plan should: diagnose the root causes of low SME access using the data available (including procurement process barriers, risk aversion, large contract packaging, and capability gaps in the department's commercial function); set annual milestones toward the 33% target; specify eight concrete policy and process changes — covering contract packaging and lot structures, financial requirements proportionality, PQQ/selection question simplification, payment terms (30-day and prompt pay code compliance), use of procurement frameworks that include SME lots, innovation challenges, and supplier development programmes; define how SME spend will be measured and reported, including the treatment of tier-2 subcontracting; identify two pilot procurements that will test the new approach before full roll-out; and assign clear internal accountability for delivery of each action.","tags":["procurement","SME","policy","commercial"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-18T10:35:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-46","title":"Public-Private Partnership Business Case","prompt":"[Describe the infrastructure project, the estimated capital cost, the public sector comparator, the key services to be provided under the PPP, and the main risks to be allocated.]\n\nDevelop a structured Public-Private Partnership (PPP) business case for the project described above. The business case should follow the Five Case Model: the Strategic Case (strategic fit, investment objectives, and the case for change); the Economic Case (options appraisal comparing do-nothing, public delivery, and PPP delivery; Public Sector Comparator; Value for Money analysis; risk-adjusted cost comparison; and sensitivity analysis); the Commercial Case (output specification approach, risk allocation matrix specifying which risks are retained by the public sector, transferred to the private sector, or shared, and the proposed payment mechanism); the Financial Case (affordability over the project lifetime, sources of funding, balance sheet treatment, and refinancing gain-sharing); and the Management Case (procurement strategy, governance arrangements, contract management resourcing, and benefits realisation plan). Include a summary recommendation and a list of the conditions that must be met before the PPP route is formally approved.","tags":["PPP","finance","business-case","infrastructure"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-18T10:40:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-47","title":"Green Procurement Policy","prompt":"You are the sustainability lead for a large metropolitan council with annual procurement spend of £520 million across categories including construction, fleet vehicles, IT, catering, cleaning, professional services, and energy. Design a comprehensive Green Procurement Policy. The policy should: set out the council's environmental procurement objectives aligned to its Net Zero 2040 commitment and Nature Recovery Strategy; specify minimum environmental requirements by procurement category — for construction (embodied carbon limits, use of certified timber, waste materials targets), for fleet (zero-emission requirement for new vehicles from 2026), for food and catering (seasonal and local sourcing, food waste reduction targets, sustainable fisheries certification), for IT (energy efficiency standards, circular economy requirements for end-of-life equipment), and for cleaning (approved product list, packaging requirements, chemical hazard limits); describe how environmental criteria will be incorporated into evaluation methodologies at different spend thresholds; define supplier reporting requirements for carbon, water, and biodiversity impacts; establish a process for engaging with suppliers who are not yet meeting requirements but have credible improvement plans; and set targets for monitoring carbon reduction across the supply chain annually.","tags":["procurement","sustainability","environment","net-zero"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-18T10:45:00Z","featured":false},

    // --- URBAN PLANNING & PUBLIC FINANCE (48-59) ---
    {"id":"gov-48","title":"Municipal Budget: Presentation Narrative","prompt":"You are the Chief Financial Officer of a city with a population of 420,000. The city council is presenting its annual budget of $1.84 billion. This year's budget reflects difficult choices: a 3.2% council tax increase (the maximum permissible); $34 million in savings achieved through service redesign; a $12 million increase in the children's social care budget to meet demand; a $9 million investment in road resurfacing funded by one-time capital receipts; and a $6 million shortfall that has been temporarily managed by drawing on reserves, which are now at their minimum prudent level. Write the budget presentation narrative (800 words) that the leader of the council will deliver at the full council meeting. The narrative must: explain the financial context honestly without catastrophising; justify the council tax increase with reference to cost pressures that are outside the council's control; explain the savings and where they have been found; make the case for the social care investment; be transparent about the reserves position and the multi-year risk it creates; and set out the council's plan to achieve structural balance over the next three years. Tone: honest, credible, and leadership-focused.","tags":["finance","budget","local-government","communications"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-19T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-49","title":"Urban Master Plan Vision Statement","prompt":"[Describe the city or district (population, current character, main challenges — for example, housing shortage, industrial decline, flood risk, rapid growth), the planning horizon, and the key outcomes the master plan should achieve.]\n\nDraft the Vision and Strategic Framework sections of an Urban Master Plan for the city or district described above. These sections should: open with an inspiring but credible vision statement for the planning horizon that describes the place the city wants to become — focusing on the lived experience of residents rather than abstract policy goals; identify five strategic spatial priorities derived from the challenges and opportunities identified, with each priority stated as a desired spatial outcome; describe the spatial strategy — how the city will grow, protect, or regenerate different areas — using a clear structure of zones or character areas; explain the relationship between housing growth, employment, transport, green infrastructure, and social infrastructure and how the plan will coordinate these; set out design principles that will guide development across all land uses; describe the approach to heritage, distinctive character, and landscape; specify the key infrastructure requirements that must be delivered to enable the plan's growth assumptions; and identify the five critical choices the plan makes and the alternatives that were considered and rejected.","tags":["urban-planning","master-plan","strategy","housing"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-19T10:05:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-50","title":"Infrastructure Investment Prioritisation","prompt":"A regional government has a five-year infrastructure capital programme of $4.2 billion and a pipeline of 38 proposed projects across transport, education, health, flood defence, housing, and digital connectivity. Not all projects can be funded. Design an infrastructure investment prioritisation framework that: defines the strategic objectives the infrastructure programme should advance (economic growth, decarbonisation, equity, resilience, and connectivity); specifies the assessment criteria and scoring methodology to evaluate each project against each objective — using a combination of quantified economic analysis (BCR, NPV) and scored qualitative assessment; explains how to account for interdependencies between projects (where building Project A makes Project B more or less valuable); incorporates distributional criteria to prevent all investment flowing to already-well-served areas; describes the governance process for applying the framework, including who scores, how disputes are resolved, and how political priorities interact with technical scoring; applies the framework illustratively to five hypothetical projects of different types; and specifies the conditions under which the framework score can be overridden by ministers and the transparency requirements when that happens.","tags":["finance","infrastructure","investment","planning"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-19T10:10:00Z","featured":true},

    {"id":"gov-51","title":"Developer Contributions Negotiation Guide","prompt":"You are a senior planning officer. Your local planning authority routinely negotiates Section 106 agreements and Community Infrastructure Levy (CIL) payments with property developers to fund affordable housing, schools, parks, transport improvements, and healthcare facilities. Write a comprehensive internal guide for planning officers on how to negotiate developer contributions effectively. The guide should cover: the legal framework and viability testing process under national planning policy; how to use development viability appraisals and when to commission an independent review of developer-submitted viability assessments; the authority's standard expectations for contributions by type of development (housing: affordable housing percentage, transport, schools, open space; commercial: transport, public realm, employment training); how to prioritise contributions when full requirements cannot be met; negotiation tactics and common developer arguments with suggested responses; the phasing of contributions and overage mechanisms; monitoring and enforcement of agreed contributions; record-keeping requirements; and how to handle disputes including referral to the Planning Inspectorate. Include a one-page reference card summarising the key steps.","tags":["planning","finance","development","negotiation"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-19T10:15:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-52","title":"Public Finance: Medium-Term Financial Strategy","prompt":"You are the Director of Finance for a national government ministry with an annual budget of $2.8 billion. The ministry has been asked by the Treasury to submit a Medium-Term Financial Strategy (MTFS) covering the next four years. The context: the ministry faces cumulative spending pressure of $340 million from demand growth, inflation, and new policy commitments; the Treasury has indicated a flat real-terms settlement is the likely outcome of the Spending Review; and the ministry has identified a pipeline of potential savings and transformation opportunities worth $180–220 million over the period, but with significant delivery risk. Write the MTFS covering: the spending baseline and how it has been constructed; the pressure analysis by category (demand, pay, inflation, new commitments) with a quantified central estimate and range; the savings pipeline with each workstream described, the expected saving, the confidence level (high/medium/low), and the earliest year of impact; sensitivity analysis showing the financial position under three scenarios (central, adverse, and stressed); the financial risks and how they will be managed; the investment needed to unlock the savings; and the four-year forward position under each scenario. Write in the precise style used in government financial planning.","tags":["finance","budget","strategy","government"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-19T10:20:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-53","title":"Climate Adaptation Plan for a City","prompt":"You are a climate resilience officer. The city of Rivermouth (population 310,000, coastal and river flood risk, 18% of buildings in flood zone, average summer temperatures projected to rise 2.4°C by 2050, urban heat island effect significant) needs a Climate Adaptation Plan to reduce its vulnerability to five climate hazards: more frequent and intense flooding, prolonged heatwaves, drought and water scarcity, increased coastal erosion, and disruption to supply chains. Write the plan's core framework sections covering: a climate risk and vulnerability assessment for each of the five hazards — describing current exposure, projected change, and which populations and assets are most at risk; a priority adaptation actions table for each hazard (at least three actions per hazard, each with a description, responsible lead, estimated cost, timeline, and co-benefit); the sequencing rationale — which actions must happen first and why; a 'no-regret' and 'low-regret' action category to guide prioritisation under uncertainty; funding sources including Green Climate Fund, UK Shared Prosperity Fund, developer contributions, and insurance mechanisms; governance and ownership; and a monitoring framework using specific, measurable climate resilience indicators.","tags":["climate","planning","resilience","environment"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-19T10:25:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-54","title":"Planning Application Decision Notice","prompt":"You are a planning officer writing the decision notice for a contested planning application. The application is for a 248-unit residential development on a brownfield site at the edge of a historic town centre. The development involves 25% affordable housing, requires demolition of a non-listed Victorian warehouse, is within 400m of a conservation area, and requires access through an existing residential street that local residents have strongly objected to. The Planning Committee has voted 6-4 to grant permission with 12 conditions. Write the full decision notice in the format required by planning regulations, covering: the decision (grant with conditions); the description of the development and the site; the list of all 12 conditions stated in complete, precise legal form (each condition must be necessary, relevant to planning, relevant to the development, enforceable, precise, and reasonable); the planning reasons for approval, addressing each material consideration including the housing land supply position, heritage impact assessment, transport assessment conclusions, affordable housing compliance, and planning history; the summary of the representations received and how each material objection was weighed; and the statement of the applicant's rights of appeal against conditions.","tags":["planning","development","decision","local-government"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-19T10:30:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-55","title":"Municipal Debt Management Policy","prompt":"You are the Treasurer of a large municipality. Design a comprehensive Debt Management Policy for the municipality, which currently carries $890 million in long-term debt across seven outstanding bond issues, has a Aa2 credit rating, and is planning $340 million in new capital borrowing over the next five years to fund infrastructure investment. The policy should cover: the purpose and objectives of debt management (cost minimisation, risk management, credit rating preservation, and intergenerational equity); the types of debt instruments the municipality is authorised to issue (general obligation bonds, revenue bonds, commercial paper, bank loans, lease financing) with the criteria for selecting between them; the authorisation process for new borrowing — who approves, at what value thresholds, and with what information requirements; interest rate risk management including the policy on fixed versus variable rate debt and the use of interest rate swap agreements; refinancing and defeasance triggers; credit rating management including the financial ratios the municipality will target and the actions to take if ratios deteriorate; disclosure obligations for bond investors; and the annual debt affordability analysis and reporting requirements to council.","tags":["finance","debt","bonds","municipal"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-19T10:35:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-56","title":"Economic Development Strategy","prompt":"[Describe the local or regional economy: population, main industries, unemployment rate, key assets (universities, ports, transport links), main challenges (industrial decline, skills gaps, geographic peripherality), and the strategic goals the economic development strategy should achieve.]\n\nDraft the core strategic framework of an Economic Development Strategy for the economy described above. The strategy should: open with an honest economic baseline — the economy's strengths, weaknesses, opportunities, and threats derived from the context provided; define a focused economic ambition for a 10-year horizon that is credible and distinctive rather than a generic aspiration; identify three to four strategic economic priorities that play to the economy's comparative advantage rather than trying to compete in sectors where it cannot win; for each priority, describe the theory of change (what market failures or barriers exist, what the strategy will do to address them, what the expected outcome is); propose a portfolio of policy interventions across business support, skills and workforce, infrastructure, innovation, and place-making, with rough cost estimates and likely funding sources; set out the governance model — who leads the strategy, how it is held accountable, and how the private sector is engaged as a genuine partner; and define six headline economic indicators that will be tracked annually to measure progress.","tags":["economics","strategy","development","local-government"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-19T10:40:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-57","title":"Housing Needs Assessment","prompt":"You are a housing policy analyst. Conduct a structured housing needs assessment for a district council area with a population of 185,000 (growing at 1.1% annually) that is characterised by high house prices relative to incomes (average house price 11x average income), a significant elderly population (24% over 65), growing numbers of single-person households, a shortage of affordable rented accommodation, and a historic undersupply against the government's local housing need figure. Write the assessment covering: demographic projections to 2040 using trend-based and scenario-based approaches; household formation projections and the resulting overall housing need figure; the breakdown of housing need by tenure (market ownership, private rented, affordable rented, shared ownership, specialist older persons' housing); the housing mix required by unit size (studio, 1-bed, 2-bed, 3-bed, 4-bed+); the affordable housing need calculated using the standard methodology including the backlog and newly arising need; specific needs assessments for self-build and custom build, Gypsy and Traveller accommodation, and specialist housing for people with disabilities; the market signals evidence; and the implications for the local plan's housing land supply requirements. Note any assumptions and their sensitivity.","tags":["housing","planning","analysis","demographics"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-19T10:45:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-58","title":"Transport Modelling Brief","prompt":"A city is proposing to introduce a new Bus Rapid Transit (BRT) corridor 14km long, connecting a major employment centre to three residential suburbs currently underserved by public transport. Before making the investment decision, the city needs transport modelling to test whether the BRT justifies the $180 million capital cost. Write a comprehensive Transport Modelling Brief that: defines the modelling objectives and the specific questions the model must answer (what change in mode shift, journey times, car trips, emissions, and development potential will the BRT deliver?); specifies the model type required (strategic multi-modal transport model, with minimum zone count and network representation standards); defines the base year and forecast year scenarios to be modelled (2025 base, 2035 and 2050 forecast) and the scenario variants (do-nothing, do-minimum, BRT as proposed, BRT with active travel integration, BRT with park-and-ride); specifies the data inputs required from the client; describes the outputs required including assigned demand, journey time savings, patronage forecasts, modal shift, and WebTAG-compliant economic values; sets quality assurance requirements; and specifies the deliverable format and presentation requirements for the decision-making body.","tags":["transport","modelling","planning","infrastructure"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-19T10:50:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-59","title":"Participatory Budgeting Programme Design","prompt":"You are a civic innovation officer. A metropolitan council wants to introduce participatory budgeting (PB) — giving citizens direct control over how a portion of the budget is spent in their neighbourhoods — as part of a broader open government agenda. The council has allocated $2.5 million for the first year of PB, to be distributed across 12 neighbourhood areas. Design the full programme including: the PB model selection — assess the trade-offs between the fund model, neighbourhood model, and city-wide model and make a recommendation; the process design covering how projects are nominated, developed, and voted on, including the timeline from launch to project approval; the community outreach and co-design approach ensuring the process reaches beyond the usual engaged citizens to include young people, people with disabilities, non-English speakers, and digitally excluded residents; the eligibility criteria for projects (what the money can and cannot be spent on); how proposals will be evaluated for technical feasibility and cost before going to a public vote; the voting mechanism (online, paper, community events) and anti-fraud safeguards; the communication plan; a proposal for how PB learning will be used to inform the main budget process; and a set of evaluation metrics to assess whether PB is achieving genuine democratic engagement.","tags":["democracy","budget","engagement","local-government"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-19T10:55:00Z","featured":false},

    // --- CITIZEN ENGAGEMENT & E-GOVERNMENT (60-70) ---
    {"id":"gov-60","title":"Citizen Engagement Strategy","prompt":"[Describe the government body (national ministry, local authority, agency), the policy area or programme where better citizen engagement is needed, the current state of engagement, and any particular demographic or community challenges.]\n\nDesign a comprehensive Citizen Engagement Strategy for the context described above. The strategy should: diagnose the current state of engagement, identifying barriers to participation (awareness, accessibility, trust, perceived impact, digital exclusion) and the communities currently most disengaged; set a clear engagement philosophy — whether the goal is consultation, co-design, or co-production, and what the government body commits to in return for citizens' time; map the engagement landscape showing which other bodies are engaging citizens on related issues to avoid consultation fatigue; propose a mixed-methods engagement programme combining large-scale digital channels (surveys, online deliberation), in-person community engagement (citizens' assemblies, neighbourhood forums, door-to-door), and creative methods (participatory mapping, photo voice, deliberative polling) with a rationale for each; specify how hard-to-reach groups will be meaningfully included with dedicated approaches and resources; describe how engagement findings will be analysed, reported back to participants, and demonstrably fed into decisions; and set a target for the proportion of the population who have meaningful engagement opportunities annually.","tags":["engagement","democracy","citizens","policy"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-20T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-61","title":"Digital Service Design: User Journey","prompt":"You are a service designer in a government digital team. Map the complete user journey for a citizen applying for a personal independence payment (PIP) — a benefit for people with long-term health conditions or disabilities. The current process involves a paper form of 34 pages, a telephone assessment, a home visit, and an average processing time of 26 weeks, with a 64% appeal success rate suggesting widespread initial incorrect decisions. Your user journey analysis should: map the current 'as-is' journey in full — every step from awareness of the benefit through to first payment or appeal outcome — identifying at each step the citizen's task, emotional state, pain points, and the government's process on the back end; identify the top 10 most severe pain points ranked by frequency and impact, with particular attention to accessibility barriers for people with cognitive, sensory, and physical impairments; design the 'to-be' improved journey with specific changes at each problematic step, explaining the design rationale; specify the digital and non-digital touchpoints in the improved journey, including the moments where human contact must be preserved; describe the accessibility requirements for any digital components; and propose five user research activities to validate the new design before it is built.","tags":["digital","service-design","accessibility","citizen-services"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-20T10:05:00Z","featured":true},

    {"id":"gov-62","title":"Open Data Strategy","prompt":"You are the Chief Data Officer of a national government. Design a comprehensive Open Data Strategy to maximise the value of government data for economic growth, public accountability, research, and improved public services. The strategy should cover: a data asset inventory approach — how to catalogue what data the government holds and assess its potential value for release; a prioritisation framework for deciding which datasets to release first (using criteria of demand, readiness for release, transformative potential, and cost of release); the legal framework for data release covering exemptions, anonymisation standards, and the data licensing model (Creative Commons, Open Government Licence); technical standards for data publication (machine-readable formats, APIs, metadata standards, data quality standards); the governance of the open data programme including who is accountable for publishing, updating, and maintaining each dataset category; a data literacy programme to help civil servants understand how to prepare data for release; a developer and innovation ecosystem strategy to stimulate use of released data; a measurement framework covering the number of datasets published, quality scores, download volumes, and documented use cases; and a five-year publication roadmap with headline targets.","tags":["open-data","transparency","digital","government"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-20T10:10:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-63","title":"GovTech Innovation Programme","prompt":"You are the Director of Digital Innovation in a national government. Design a GovTech Innovation Programme to identify, test, and scale technology solutions developed by startups and SMEs that address priority government challenges. The programme should cover: the challenge areas to be addressed in the first cohort — select five from: AI for fraud detection, accessibility tools for people with disabilities, predictive analytics for preventive health interventions, carbon accounting tools for the public estate, and automated document processing for back-office functions; the programme model — challenge-based procurement, innovation partnership, or incubator approach — with rationale; the application and selection process for technology companies, including how to encourage applications from diverse founders and non-metropolitan companies; the prototyping and pilot phase design including what the government commits to provide (data access, civil servant time, procurement pathway) and what it expects from companies; intellectual property arrangements; the route from successful pilot to procurement at scale and the barriers that commonly prevent this transition; governance and accountability; and the evaluation framework. Include a letter of invitation to the market explaining the programme.","tags":["innovation","digital","procurement","government"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-20T10:15:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-64","title":"Algorithmic Accountability Framework","prompt":"A national welfare agency uses three algorithmic decision-support tools: (1) a fraud detection algorithm that flags benefit claims for investigation; (2) a prioritisation model that ranks housing applicants for available social housing units; (3) a predictive model that identifies children at risk of abuse to trigger early intervention. Each tool was developed by a third-party supplier. Write a comprehensive Algorithmic Accountability Framework that applies to all three tools and any future algorithmic systems. The framework must address: a register of algorithmic systems with required documentation for each (purpose, data inputs, model type, decision role, supplier, performance metrics, last audit date); a pre-deployment audit process covering bias testing across protected characteristics, explainability requirements, and the minimum human oversight requirements for each risk tier; ongoing monitoring requirements including performance drift detection and regular bias re-testing; transparency and explainability requirements for decisions made with algorithmic assistance — what must citizens be told, in what form, and when; the right to a meaningful human review of algorithmic-influenced decisions; procurement requirements for externally developed systems including source code access rights and audit cooperation obligations; the oversight governance including an internal algorithm ethics panel and independent external audit; and a complaints and redress pathway.","tags":["AI","ethics","accountability","government"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-20T10:20:00Z","featured":true},

    {"id":"gov-65","title":"Digital Inclusion Action Plan","prompt":"You are the lead for digital inclusion in a metropolitan authority. An analysis of the borough's population shows that 19% of residents (42,000 people) lack basic digital skills, 14% lack home internet access, and a further 8% have devices but lack the confidence to use online services effectively. The council is moving 35% of its services to digital-first delivery over the next two years. Write a Digital Inclusion Action Plan that ensures vulnerable residents are not left behind. The plan should cover: a segmentation of the digitally excluded population by barrier type (no access, no skills, no confidence, no motivation) and the priority groups requiring targeted support (elderly residents living alone, disabled people, low-income families, recent migrants, people with low literacy); partnership arrangements with libraries, housing associations, community organisations, and telecom providers; the device lending programme — target scale, device specifications, data security for returned devices, and sustainability; the skills and confidence training programme including curriculum outline for beginners and advanced levels, volunteer tutor recruitment and training, and accessible venue requirements; the assisted digital service — how citizens who cannot use digital services unaided will continue to receive equivalent quality of service; a target to reduce the digitally excluded population by a specific percentage over two years; and an evaluation approach.","tags":["digital-inclusion","accessibility","citizen-services","policy"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-20T10:25:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-66","title":"Citizens' Assembly Design","prompt":"A national government is considering fundamental reform of its pension system — a politically contentious issue where public opinion is complex and any reform will involve trade-offs between current and future retirees, between different income groups, and between fiscal sustainability and adequacy of retirement income. The government wants to use a citizens' assembly to inform the reform, rather than simply subject it to normal political debate. Design the citizens' assembly in full, covering: the mandate and question the assembly will address (the specific decision or recommendation it will be asked to make); the selection methodology for 100 participants to be demographically representative by age, gender, region, socioeconomic status, and ethnicity; the assembly schedule — number of weekends, topics for each session, balance between expert testimony, facilitated deliberation, and participant-led discussion; the information and evidence programme — which experts and stakeholders will present, how balance will be ensured, and how participants will access independent information; the deliberation methodology including small group facilitation, visual harvesting, and plenary sessions; the outputs expected — a final report, voted recommendations, and a dissenting views record; how the government will respond to the assembly's recommendations and commit to accountability; and the communications strategy to maintain public trust in the process.","tags":["democracy","engagement","deliberation","policy"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-20T10:30:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-67","title":"Smart City Data Governance Framework","prompt":"A major city is rolling out a network of 4,200 IoT sensors across streets, parks, public buildings, and transport infrastructure. The sensors collect data including pedestrian counts, vehicle flow, air quality measurements, noise levels, bin fill rates, and — through CCTV integration — object detection in public spaces. Design a Smart City Data Governance Framework that maximises the operational and planning value of this data while protecting civil liberties and maintaining public trust. The framework must cover: data minimisation principles — what data should and should not be collected, retention periods by data type, and the automatic deletion protocol; privacy by design requirements for sensor procurement; the distinction between genuinely anonymised aggregate data and pseudonymous or identifiable data, and the different governance rules that apply to each; purpose limitation — what the data can and cannot be used for, with particular rules around law enforcement access; transparency requirements including a public register of all sensors with their location, data collected, and retention period; the oversight body for approving new sensor deployments; independent audit requirements; citizen rights of access and objection; and a prohibited uses list (facial recognition in public spaces, individual behaviour profiling, data sale to commercial third parties).","tags":["smart-city","data","privacy","governance"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-20T10:35:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-68","title":"Public Sector AI Procurement Guide","prompt":"You are a senior technology adviser in a central government digital authority. Write a practical guide for public sector procurement officers on how to buy AI systems responsibly. The guide should cover, in plain English accessible to non-technical procurement officers: a brief taxonomy of AI system types they are likely to encounter (rule-based automation, machine learning, large language models, computer vision) and the different risks each presents; a risk tiering methodology to classify AI systems by potential harm — high risk (AI making or significantly influencing decisions about individuals), medium risk (AI supporting decisions with significant oversight), and low risk (AI for internal productivity); the mandatory information to request from AI suppliers at the RFP stage, including training data provenance, model performance benchmarks, bias testing methodology and results, explainability capabilities, human oversight design, security and data handling, and ongoing monitoring commitments; contract clauses to include for AI systems including audit rights, model change notification, performance SLAs, and exit data provisions; due diligence questions for evaluators who are not AI specialists; red flags that should disqualify a supplier or require escalation; and references to the EU AI Act, the UK AI regulation approach, and relevant sector-specific guidance.","tags":["AI","procurement","digital","government"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-20T10:40:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-69","title":"E-Government Maturity Assessment","prompt":"[Describe the government body to be assessed and provide any available information about its current digital services, IT infrastructure, data capabilities, and digital workforce.]\n\nConduct a structured E-Government Maturity Assessment for the body described above, using a five-level maturity model: Level 1 — Presence (basic informational website); Level 2 — Interaction (downloadable forms, email contact); Level 3 — Transaction (end-to-end digital service completion); Level 4 — Transformation (integrated, personalised, proactive services); Level 5 — Intelligent Government (AI-driven, anticipatory services, full data integration). The assessment should: evaluate the organisation's current maturity level across six dimensions — citizen services, back-office digitisation, data and analytics capability, cybersecurity posture, digital workforce skills, and governance and leadership; produce a maturity scorecard showing current level and target level for each dimension; identify the three most critical gaps that are limiting overall maturity; propose a prioritised two-year roadmap to advance maturity with specific initiatives, estimated costs, dependencies, and the maturity level each initiative unlocks; and identify the governance, cultural, and skills changes required alongside technology investment. Conclude with three benchmark comparisons from leading digital governments.","tags":["e-government","digital","maturity","assessment"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-20T10:45:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-70","title":"Government CRM Implementation Plan","prompt":"A large national employment service wants to implement a Customer Relationship Management (CRM) system to give frontline advisers a single view of each jobseeker's journey — combining data from their initial registration, benefit claims, skills assessments, job applications, employer contacts, and programme participation. The service has 4,800 advisers across 180 job centres and a legacy system that is 16 years old. Write an implementation plan covering: the business case summary and expected benefits (reduced duplicate data entry, better-targeted support, improved employment outcomes); requirements gathering process — how to capture adviser, manager, and senior leader requirements while avoiding scope creep; a vendor selection approach including key evaluation criteria for a CRM system in this context; data migration strategy for 2.3 million active claimant records from the legacy system including data quality remediation; change management and training plan for 4,800 advisers — how to sequence training, handle resistance, and build internal champions; a phased rollout plan across 180 sites with go/no-go criteria for each phase; the testing strategy including user acceptance testing with advisers and integration testing with benefit payment systems; and the post-implementation support model and benefits realisation review.","tags":["digital","CRM","implementation","employment"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-20T10:50:00Z","featured":false},

    // --- PUBLIC SECTOR HR & MANAGEMENT (71-80) ---
    {"id":"gov-71","title":"Public Sector Workforce Strategy","prompt":"[Describe the government organisation, its size and workforce profile, the strategic challenges it faces over the next five years (demographic change, skills gaps, digital transformation, budget pressures), and any specific workforce issues such as high turnover, low diversity, or cultural problems.]\n\nDevelop a comprehensive Workforce Strategy for the organisation described above covering a five-year horizon. The strategy should include: a workforce analysis covering current headcount, grade distribution, age profile, diversity data, turnover rates, and skills profile — identifying the critical gaps between current capability and what will be needed to deliver the organisation's strategy; a future workforce model specifying the skills, roles, and size of the workforce needed in five years under different scenarios; a resourcing and pipeline strategy covering recruitment, grow-your-own talent development, targeted retention of critical roles, and an honest assessment of outsourcing versus insourcing trade-offs; a diversity, equity, and inclusion plan with specific targets, accountability, and evidence-based interventions; a learning and development framework specifying the investment per employee, the priority skills areas, and the blend of formal training, mentoring, coaching, and on-the-job development; a wellbeing strategy covering physical, mental, and financial wellbeing; leadership development pipeline; a cultural change programme if relevant; and a workforce planning governance model with annual review checkpoints.","tags":["HR","workforce","strategy","government"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-21T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-72","title":"Performance Management Framework","prompt":"You are the HR Director of a national government department of 3,200 staff. The current performance management system — an annual appraisal with a five-point rating scale — is widely regarded as ineffective: 91% of staff receive a 3 ('meets expectations') rating regardless of actual performance, poor performance is rarely addressed, and the process is seen as a bureaucratic exercise rather than a genuine development tool. Redesign the performance management framework. The new framework should: articulate a clear philosophy — what the organisation believes about performance management and what it is for; describe the annual cycle of performance conversations (not just one annual review but quarterly conversations with specific purposes); redesign the objective-setting process to produce objectives that are genuinely stretching, clearly linked to organisational priorities, and developed through dialogue rather than imposed top-down; reform the assessment process to address rating inflation — with guidance for managers on making honest assessments and accountability for managers who consistently rate all staff at the midpoint; define a clear process for recognising and rewarding strong performance within civil service pay constraints; create an early intervention framework for underperformance that focuses on support before formal process; and specify the manager training required to make the new framework work.","tags":["HR","performance","management","government"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-21T10:05:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-73","title":"Public Sector Diversity and Inclusion Strategy","prompt":"You are the Head of Diversity and Inclusion for a government ministry with 5,400 staff. The ministry's latest diversity data shows: 13% Black and Minority Ethnic (BME) staff overall but only 4% at Senior Civil Service level; 38% women overall but 29% at Senior Civil Service level; 6% declared disability, likely an undercount due to low disclosure confidence; BME staff are 2.4x more likely than white staff to face formal disciplinary action; and staff survey scores for inclusion are 12 points lower for BME staff than for white staff. Write a three-year Diversity and Inclusion Strategy that goes beyond aspirational statements to drive measurable change. The strategy should: acknowledge the data honestly without minimising the seriousness of the disciplinary disparity; set specific, time-bound targets for representation at each grade level; identify the structural and cultural barriers that evidence suggests cause the representation and inclusion gaps; propose a portfolio of evidence-based interventions across five domains — data and transparency, recruitment and selection, progression and sponsorship, culture and inclusion, and disciplinary equity; assign accountability for each intervention to a named grade of leader; specify the monitoring and reporting rhythm; and describe the governance and external scrutiny to hold the ministry accountable.","tags":["diversity","inclusion","HR","government"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-21T10:10:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-74","title":"Organisational Redesign Business Case","prompt":"A government agency responsible for issuing business licences and managing commercial compliance has 840 staff, is significantly behind on its licence processing backlog (230,000 applications pending, average wait time 18 weeks against a statutory 8-week target), and has a unit cost that is 3x higher than the equivalent function in comparable countries. A management review has identified three organisational design options: Option A — status quo with process improvement; Option B — restructure into two specialist directorates (licensing and enforcement) with a shared services function; Option C — full digitalisation of licensing with a reduced workforce of 320 focused on complex cases and enforcement. Write a full organisational redesign business case covering: the case for change with quantified evidence of current underperformance; the three options with detailed description of the operating model, structure, and ways of working for each; the comparative assessment against cost, quality, staff impact, implementation risk, and strategic fit criteria; the recommended option with full rationale; implementation plan including transition management, redundancy and redeployment plan, and union consultation approach; investment required and financial case; governance; and the success metrics that will confirm the redesign has achieved its objectives.","tags":["organisational-design","HR","restructure","government"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-21T10:15:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-75","title":"Whistleblowing Policy and Culture Programme","prompt":"You are the integrity lead for a large government ministry. The ministry's current whistleblowing policy is a four-page document that has not been updated in seven years. Only 12 whistleblowing concerns were formally raised last year — a number that internal research suggests significantly understates the actual level of wrongdoing observed by staff. Staff survey data shows that only 38% of staff believe they would be protected if they raised a concern, and only 29% believe concerns are taken seriously. Write a comprehensive reformed Whistleblowing Policy and accompanying Culture Change Programme. The policy should: clearly define what types of concerns qualify for protected disclosure (corruption, criminal conduct, health and safety risks, financial irregularity, abuse of power, cover-ups); describe the range of channels for raising concerns — direct line management, the senior nominated whistleblowing officer, a confidential hotline, and an external route to the relevant Ombudsman or regulator; specify the protections against detriment and the explicit prohibition on retaliation, with named accountability; describe the investigation process and the feedback commitment to the discloser; and set out the annual governance report to the Accounting Officer. The culture programme should address manager capability, tone from the top, and the metrics used to assess whether culture is genuinely improving.","tags":["integrity","HR","accountability","governance"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-21T10:20:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-76","title":"Public Service Delivery Review","prompt":"[Describe the public service to be reviewed — what it does, who it serves, how it is delivered, the scale of operation, and the specific concerns or questions that have prompted the review.]\n\nConduct a structured Public Service Delivery Review of the service described above. The review should: establish the review's scope, methodology, and the evidence base used; assess the service against four dimensions — effectiveness (is the service achieving its intended outcomes for users?), efficiency (is the service delivering value for money?), equity (are outcomes equitable across different population groups?), and experience (do users find the service accessible, respectful, and responsive?); benchmark the service's performance against comparable services in three other jurisdictions, identifying the performance gap and the drivers behind higher-performing comparators; identify the top five delivery improvement opportunities with an estimate of the potential improvement in outcomes or cost; present user research findings — the lived experience of service users, with particular attention to the experience of vulnerable groups and those whose needs are currently unmet; make eight prioritised recommendations covering service design, workforce, technology, commissioning, and governance; and propose a delivery improvement programme with milestones over 18 months.","tags":["service-delivery","review","performance","government"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-21T10:25:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-77","title":"Civil Service Learning and Development Curriculum","prompt":"You are the Head of Learning and Development for a central government department of 2,800 staff. Design a comprehensive Learning and Development Curriculum aligned to the Civil Service Competency Framework. The curriculum should be tiered by grade: entry level (administrative officers and assistants), middle management (executive officers and higher executive officers), and senior leadership (Grade 7, Grade 6, and Senior Civil Service). For each tier, specify: the core curriculum (mandatory learning that all staff at that level must complete — covering policy skills, digital literacy, data analysis, financial management, commercial awareness, and communication); the leadership and management pathway (for those who manage others); specialist pathways for the most common professional disciplines in the department (policy, digital, finance, HR, commercial); development experiences beyond formal training (job shadowing, secondments, stretch assignments, coaching, mentoring, peer learning networks); the annual learning hours target and how time for learning will be protected; the use of digital learning platforms versus face-to-face provision; the approach to identifying high-potential staff; and the evaluation framework to assess whether learning is translating into improved performance.","tags":["learning","HR","capability","civil-service"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-21T10:30:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-78","title":"Hybrid Working Policy for Public Sector","prompt":"A national government ministry (3,400 staff, six offices, a mix of policy, operational, and digital roles) is developing a hybrid working policy following the transition from full remote working during the pandemic. The policy must balance employee flexibility and wellbeing, service delivery requirements, the minister's expectation that civil servants are visible in offices, equity between roles that can be done remotely and those that cannot, and the need to maintain a strong organisational culture and knowledge transfer. Write a comprehensive Hybrid Working Policy covering: the definition of hybrid working and the guiding principles; role categorisation — which roles require full office presence, which are eligible for hybrid working, and the criteria used to make that determination; the minimum in-office expectation for hybrid-eligible roles — expressed as days or proportion of time rather than specific days to allow team flexibility; the process for agreeing individual hybrid working arrangements — manager discretion versus entitlement, the role of team agreements, and what happens when a team agreement cannot be reached; reasonable adjustment provisions for disabled staff; the manager's role and what support managers need; equipment and home working standards; performance management in a hybrid environment; the review process; and the equity provisions ensuring staff who must work on-site are not disadvantaged in career progression.","tags":["HR","hybrid-working","policy","civil-service"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-21T10:35:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-79","title":"Public Sector Leadership Development Programme","prompt":"You are a senior leadership development adviser. Design a 12-month senior leadership development programme for 24 participants who are mid-career civil servants (Grade 7 and Grade 6 equivalent) identified as high potential for Senior Civil Service roles within three to five years. The programme should develop the specific capabilities most critical for senior public sector leadership: strategic thinking and systems leadership, managing in conditions of ambiguity and political complexity, leading transformational change, building and sustaining high-performing diverse teams, stakeholder influence and communication, commercial and financial acumen, and personal resilience and wellbeing. The programme design should include: a learning architecture diagram showing how the 12 months are structured; the specific learning interventions — residential modules (content and methods), action learning sets, coaching, mentoring by SCS leaders, cross-department and cross-sector secondments, and a capstone project; how the programme will maintain diversity and prevent the homogenisation of leadership style; the assessment approach — how progress will be observed and how the programme will identify those who are genuinely ready for promotion versus those who need further development; the governance model; estimated cost per participant; and the evaluation methodology to demonstrate return on investment.","tags":["leadership","HR","development","civil-service"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-21T10:40:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-80","title":"Change Management Plan for Government Transformation","prompt":"[Describe the transformation programme — what is changing, why, the scale of the organisation affected, the timeline, and the main sources of resistance or concern among staff and stakeholders.]\n\nDevelop a comprehensive Change Management Plan for the transformation described above. The plan should: articulate the case for change in language that resonates with the people most affected — not corporate strategy language, but a clear answer to 'why must we change, why now, and what happens if we don't?'; conduct a change impact assessment covering which groups are most affected, the magnitude of change they face, the losses they will experience (certainty, skills relevance, relationships, identity), and the likely emotional journey; design the leadership engagement strategy — what senior leaders need to do, say, and model to lead the change credibly; develop a stakeholder engagement plan with specific approaches for the most influential sceptics; design a communication strategy that follows the principle of 'tell them what's happening, why, what it means for them, and what they can do'; specify the support and capability building required to help staff navigate the transition; create a listening and feedback mechanism that can detect resistance early and adapt the change approach; identify the quick wins that will demonstrate early momentum; and define a benefits realisation tracker that measures the change outcomes that matter to staff and citizens, not just process milestones.","tags":["change-management","transformation","HR","government"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-21T10:45:00Z","featured":false},

    // --- INTERGOVERNMENTAL RELATIONS, OPEN GOVERNMENT & REGULATORY (81-100) ---
    {"id":"gov-81","title":"Intergovernmental Agreement Framework","prompt":"[Describe the two or more levels of government that need to coordinate (e.g. federal and state, national and municipal), the policy area requiring coordination, the key points of tension or misalignment, and the desired outcome of the agreement.]\n\nDraft a comprehensive Intergovernmental Agreement (IGA) framework for the coordination described above. The IGA should include: a preamble setting out the constitutional basis for the agreement and the shared commitment to the purpose it serves; a definitions section; a statement of shared objectives expressed as measurable outcomes rather than activities; the role and responsibilities of each party at each level of government, resolving the tensions identified through clarity about who leads, who contributes, and who is consulted on each type of decision; fiscal arrangements specifying funding flows, grant conditions, accountability for expenditure, and the consequences of non-compliance with conditions; a data sharing protocol covering what data flows between levels, on what legal basis, under what confidentiality requirements, and with what security standards; a dispute resolution mechanism — starting with bilateral negotiation, escalating to mediated discussion, and providing for arbitration as a last resort; amendment and renewal procedures; reporting and joint accountability requirements; and an exit and wind-down provision. Note the areas of the agreement that are likely to require the most intensive negotiation and why.","tags":["intergovernmental","federalism","coordination","governance"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-22T10:00:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-82","title":"Devolution Business Case","prompt":"A national government is considering devolving control over skills and employment policy to a group of seven city-regions that have requested greater autonomy. The city-regions argue that local control will produce better employment outcomes by aligning skills provision to local labour market needs. The national government is concerned about equity (ensuring residents in poorer city-regions receive equivalent services), accountability for public funds, and maintaining national standards. Write a devolution business case that: makes the theoretical and empirical case for devolution in the skills and employment domain, drawing on academic evidence and international comparisons; quantifies the potential economic benefit of better-aligned skills policy using plausible assumptions; maps the governance architecture of the proposed devolved system — what powers are transferred, what are retained nationally, and what are shared; describes the accountability framework for devolved powers including the scrutiny arrangements, audit regime, and consequences of poor performance; proposes the transition arrangements — how capability will be built in city-regions before full devolution and what the de-volution trigger points are; sets out the deal conditions that city-regions must meet to access devolved powers; and honestly assesses the risks of devolution with proposed mitigations. Make a recommendation on whether to proceed.","tags":["devolution","federalism","policy","governance"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-22T10:05:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-83","title":"Open Government Partnership National Action Plan","prompt":"You are a senior official preparing your country's fourth National Action Plan (NAP) for the Open Government Partnership (OGP). The OGP requires NAPs to contain SMART commitments on transparency, accountability, and citizen participation. Previous NAPs have been criticised for containing commitments that were already underway, lacked ambition, and were developed without genuine civil society co-creation. Write a model NAP containing six commitments. For each commitment: specify the problem it addresses and the open government value it advances (transparency, accountability, participation, technology and innovation); describe the commitment in full — what will be done, by whom, by when, with what milestone structure; explain how the commitment was co-created with civil society; set the baseline (what currently exists) and the target (what will exist by the end of the plan); identify the responsible ministry and named senior official; describe how progress will be independently monitored by civil society; and explain how the commitment goes beyond what would happen anyway without the OGP process. At least two commitments should address access to information, two should address fiscal transparency, and two should address participatory governance.","tags":["open-government","transparency","accountability","OGP"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-22T10:10:00Z","featured":true},

    {"id":"gov-84","title":"Parliamentary Accountability Strengthening Report","prompt":"You are an expert on parliamentary accountability systems. A parliament's Public Accounts Committee (PAC) has asked you to write a report assessing the effectiveness of its financial oversight function and recommending reforms to strengthen it. The parliament has 120 members, the PAC has 11 members and a staff of 8, it holds 22 evidence sessions per year, it receives qualified audit opinions on 14% of government accounts, and its recommendations are implemented by government at a rate of only 43% — significantly below the comparable figure of 68% in peer parliaments. Write the report covering: an assessment of the current strengths and weaknesses of the PAC's work using the four accountability functions — audit, inquiry, recommendation, and follow-up; analysis of why recommendation implementation rates are low — is it the quality of the recommendations, the government's response process, or the follow-up mechanism?; international best practice from the UK National Audit Office, Canadian Auditor General, and Australian Joint Committee of Public Accounts and Audit; ten specific recommendations to strengthen the PAC's effectiveness, covering staffing, evidence-gathering powers, follow-up mechanisms, relationship with the Supreme Audit Institution, and engagement with civil society; and an implementation prioritisation with a two-year roadmap.","tags":["parliament","accountability","audit","governance"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-22T10:15:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-85","title":"Anti-Corruption Strategy","prompt":"[Describe the country or jurisdiction, the main forms of corruption that are most prevalent (grand corruption, petty administrative corruption, procurement fraud, political financing violations), the existing legal and institutional framework, and the main weaknesses in the current anti-corruption architecture.]\n\nDraft a comprehensive Anti-Corruption Strategy for the context described above, covering a five-year period. The strategy should: open with an honest diagnosis of the corruption problem — its nature, scale, economic cost, and societal impact — avoiding the typical government tendency to minimise the problem; set out a theory of change explaining how the strategy's interventions will reduce corruption across three levels (individual incentives, institutional controls, and social norms); propose reforms across five pillars — prevention (procurement controls, conflict of interest management, asset declarations, lobbying transparency), detection (audit and oversight capacity, whistleblowing, investigative journalism support, financial intelligence), prosecution (independence and capacity of the anti-corruption agency and courts), recovery (asset recovery legislation and international cooperation), and civil society and public accountability (freedom of information, public sector transparency, civic education); set specific targets for each pillar with baseline data; define the governance of the strategy including an independent monitoring mechanism that includes civil society; and address the implementation risks including political capture of the anti-corruption agency.","tags":["anti-corruption","governance","integrity","policy"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-22T10:20:00Z","featured":true},

    {"id":"gov-86","title":"Government Ombudsman: Casework Report","prompt":"You are the Head of Casework for a national Parliamentary and Health Service Ombudsman. Write a model annual casework report covering 12 months of complaint handling. The report should: present the year's complaint data — total complaints received (18,400), complaints investigated (2,240), and the breakdown by body investigated (NHS: 41%, Department for Work and Pensions: 22%, Home Office: 18%, other departments: 19%); present the outcome data — upheld in full (31%), partially upheld (28%), not upheld (41%) — and the year-on-year trends; provide three anonymised but detailed case studies — one upheld complaint demonstrating systemic failure and the improvements secured, one partially upheld case illustrating the balance between citizen and government, and one not upheld case demonstrating fair treatment of a genuinely challenging complainant; report on whether government bodies are implementing Ombudsman recommendations — include the three bodies with the highest compliance rates and the two with the lowest; present the themes emerging across multiple complaints that suggest systemic issues warranting a thematic investigation; report on the Ombudsman's own performance against its service standards; and set out the priorities for the year ahead.","tags":["ombudsman","accountability","complaints","government"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-22T10:25:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-87","title":"Fiscal Transparency Report","prompt":"You are the Director of Budget at a finance ministry. The government has committed to the IMF's Fiscal Transparency Code and has been assessed as reaching basic practice level. The ministry wants to advance to good and advanced practice levels within three years. Write a Fiscal Transparency Improvement Plan covering the four pillars of the Fiscal Transparency Code. Pillar 1 (Fiscal Reporting) — assess current practice against the standards for coverage of public sector entities, frequency and timeliness of fiscal reports, and quality assurance, then propose specific improvements; Pillar 2 (Fiscal Forecasting and Budgeting) — assess the comprehensiveness of the budget documentation, the quality of fiscal forecasts, and the medium-term budget framework, then propose reforms including a Charter for Budget Responsibility; Pillar 3 (Fiscal Risk Analysis and Management) — assess how fiscal risks (contingent liabilities, PPP commitments, natural disaster exposure, banking sector vulnerabilities) are disclosed and managed, then propose a Fiscal Risk Statement; Pillar 4 (Resource Revenue Management) — if applicable to the jurisdiction, assess transparency of extractive industry revenues against EITI standards. For each pillar, specify the current practice level, the target level, the actions required, responsible officials, and the timeline.","tags":["finance","transparency","budget","government"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-22T10:30:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-88","title":"Government Project Management Standard","prompt":"You are the head of a government Major Projects Authority. Government projects have a poor track record: the last Parliamentary review identified that 42% of major projects were red-rated on time, 38% on budget, and 27% on benefits realisation. Design a Government Project Management Standard that all major projects (over $50 million) must comply with. The standard should cover: the project lifecycle — gate definitions, the information required at each gate review, and the criteria for passing each gate; the mandatory governance structure including the role of a Senior Responsible Owner (non-delegable accountability), a project board, and independent assurance; the project business case standard — the five-case model with specific requirements at each stage of development; the integrated planning requirements covering WBS, critical path, resource plan, and risk and issue registers; the project controls minimum standards — earned value management, schedule performance index, and cost performance index thresholds that trigger escalation; the independent assurance regime — who commissions it, what form it takes, and how findings are escalated; the project data requirements feeding a central portfolio dashboard; the learning and knowledge management requirements at project close; and the consequences for non-compliance.","tags":["project-management","governance","government","standards"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-22T10:35:00Z","featured":true},

    {"id":"gov-89","title":"Government Risk Management Framework","prompt":"You are the Chief Risk Officer of a government ministry. The ministry currently has a perfunctory risk management process — risk registers are maintained as a compliance exercise, risks are not escalated in a timely way, and three significant risks materialised last year without adequate warning to senior leaders. Design a comprehensive Government Risk Management Framework. The framework should cover: the risk management philosophy and what it is trying to achieve (not compliance theatre, but genuinely better decisions); the risk taxonomy for the ministry covering strategic, operational, financial, reputational, legal, and security risk categories with examples; the risk assessment methodology including a 5x5 likelihood-impact matrix with precise anchoring descriptions for each level (avoiding the usual vague language that makes scoring inconsistent); risk appetite statements — what level of risk is the ministry willing to accept in each category and how that is expressed clearly enough to guide operational decisions; the risk ownership model — who owns risks at each level, the accountability at Board and Accounting Officer level, and the escalation triggers; the governance rhythm including monthly risk reviews, quarterly deep dives, and the connection between risk and planning cycles; early warning indicators for the ministry's top 10 risks; the assurance mapping approach; and a cultural change plan to move from compliance-based to genuine risk management.","tags":["risk","governance","management","government"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-22T10:40:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-90","title":"Government Communications Review","prompt":"A government's Central Communications Directorate (CCD) has an annual budget of $85 million, 340 staff, and coordinates communications activity across 22 departments and agencies. An independent review has been commissioned following criticism that government communications are too centralised and slow, that departments cannot respond quickly to emerging issues, that there is inadequate evaluation of campaign effectiveness, and that the CCD has prioritised ministerial interests over the public interest in its media handling. Write the review report covering: a diagnostic assessment of the CCD's current structure, operating model, and culture against the five functions of government communications (information provision, behaviour change, crisis communications, internal communications, and stakeholder engagement); benchmarking against comparable government communications operations in three countries; the specific findings under each of the four criticisms raised; eight recommendations covering structure, governance, decision-making processes, campaign evaluation standards, a renewed ethics framework for media handling, and the relationship between political advisers and permanent communications staff; and a change management approach for implementing the recommendations without losing effective coordination capability.","tags":["communications","review","government","management"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-22T10:45:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-91","title":"Regulatory Review: Cutting Red Tape","prompt":"You are a senior official leading a Better Regulation initiative. The government has committed to reducing the administrative burden on businesses by 25% over three years, without weakening substantive protections for consumers, workers, and the environment. Design the Regulatory Review Programme. The programme should: define 'administrative burden' precisely — distinguishing compliance costs that are inherent to achieving regulatory objectives from costs that exist purely because of how the requirement is administered; describe the methodology for measuring the current administrative burden — using a Standard Cost Model and stakeholder consultation — and setting a credible baseline; identify the categories of regulatory requirement most likely to yield high burden reduction with low policy risk: reporting requirements, approval and licensing processes, record-keeping requirements, and notification obligations; specify the process for departments to review their regulatory portfolio and identify burden reduction opportunities; describe the stakeholder engagement programme to identify burden from business, voluntary sector, and citizen perspectives; establish a 'one-in, two-out' or equivalent rule for new regulatory requirements; design the scrutiny process ensuring that burden reduction proposals do not inadvertently weaken protections for vulnerable groups; and set up a monitoring and reporting framework tracking progress toward the 25% target.","tags":["regulation","red-tape","business","policy"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-22T10:50:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-92","title":"Audit Committee Terms of Reference","prompt":"You are a governance adviser helping a major public sector organisation (a national health authority, annual budget $1.1 billion, 8,400 staff) establish an Audit and Risk Assurance Committee as a non-executive governance body providing independent assurance to the Accounting Officer and Board. Draft comprehensive Terms of Reference for the Committee covering: the committee's purpose, status, and relationship to the Board; membership requirements — the number of non-executive members, the requirement for financial expertise, independence requirements, term limits, and how members are appointed and removed; the scope of the committee's assurance role — covering financial reporting, internal audit, external audit, risk management, counter fraud, information governance, and clinical governance assurance; the annual work programme including minimum meeting frequency, standing agenda items, and the planning cycle for the year's assurance activities; the relationship with internal audit — how the committee approves the internal audit plan, receives findings, monitors management actions, and assesses the head of internal audit's independence; the relationship with the external auditor; reporting arrangements to the Board; how the committee conducts its own effectiveness review; and the escalation procedure when the committee identifies a matter of serious concern.","tags":["governance","audit","accountability","public-sector"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-22T10:55:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-93","title":"Lobbying Transparency Regulation","prompt":"You are a policy adviser on integrity in public life. The government wants to introduce a statutory lobbying register to improve transparency about who is trying to influence government decisions and on behalf of whom. Draft a Lobbying Transparency Act outline covering: the definitions of 'lobbying activity' and 'lobbyist' — drawing the line between professional lobbying that should be registered and activities that should be exempt (in-house government affairs staff, charities communicating beneficiary interests, informal public contact); which contacts must be registered — specifying the seniority of officials contacted, the nature of the communication, and whether written and digital communications are included as well as in-person meetings; the information required in the register — name, client, subject matter, date and parties of meetings — at a level of detail that is genuinely informative to the public; the frequency and timeline for disclosure; the enforcement body, its powers, and the penalty structure; the transparency obligations on government officials receiving lobbying contacts — particularly the 'revolving door' between senior officials and the lobbying industry; a cooling-off period for former ministers and senior officials before they can lobby their former department; and a sunset review after three years.","tags":["transparency","lobbying","integrity","legislation"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-22T11:00:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-94","title":"Government Innovation Lab Design","prompt":"You are an innovation policy director. Design a government innovation lab that will test and develop new approaches to persistent public sector challenges that conventional government has failed to solve. The lab should operate within a national health and social care system. Write the full design document covering: the lab's mission and the types of problems it will work on — focus on intractable system-level challenges where incremental improvement is insufficient; the operational model — embedded in government or arm's-length, and the governance relationship with ministers and officials; the team composition and the unusual mix of skills required (designers, data scientists, ethnographers, social workers, behavioural scientists, policy generalists); the innovation methodology — how the lab moves from problem discovery through insight, ideation, prototyping, testing, and scaling, and the specific tools and methods used at each stage; the portfolio management approach — how many concurrent projects, how resource is allocated, and the kill criteria for projects that are not working; how the lab maintains independence while being embedded enough to influence mainstream policy; the intellectual property and knowledge management regime; the approach to scaling innovations from lab to system; and an honest assessment of the conditions required for a government innovation lab to be effective rather than performative.","tags":["innovation","policy","design","government"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-22T11:05:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-95","title":"Government Benchmarking Study","prompt":"[Describe the government function or service to be benchmarked (e.g. tax collection efficiency, social care outcomes, planning decision speed), the jurisdiction commissioning the study, and the three to five comparator jurisdictions to be included.]\n\nConduct a structured government benchmarking study for the function and comparators described above. The study should: define the benchmarking scope precisely — what is being measured and what is explicitly out of scope; develop an indicator framework with 8–12 quantitative indicators that capture the performance of the function across dimensions of efficiency, effectiveness, quality, equity, and innovation; collect and present data for each jurisdiction against each indicator in a clearly formatted comparison table, noting data source, vintage, and any methodological caveats that affect comparability; conduct a contextual analysis for each comparator identifying the factors that most plausibly explain performance differences — including funding levels, population characteristics, legislative framework, historical trajectory, and governance model; identify the top three performance improvement opportunities for the commissioning jurisdiction, with an assessment of transferability; produce a summary scorecard showing overall performance ranking and the dimensions of greatest strength and weakness; and recommend four specific learning visits or knowledge exchange activities that would build on the benchmarking findings.","tags":["benchmarking","performance","international","government"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-22T11:10:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-96","title":"Government Programme Spending Review Submission","prompt":"You are the director responsible for a major government programme — a national childcare expansion programme with an annual budget of $420 million covering free childcare entitlements, capital grants for new nursery places, and a workforce development fund. A Spending Review is underway and HM Treasury has asked departments to model three scenarios: flat real-terms settlement (0% real increase), 5% real-terms reduction, and 10% real-terms reduction. Write the programme's Spending Review submission covering: the programme's strategic importance and alignment to government priorities; the evidence base for the programme's impact — what outcomes it delivers and the economic return; the current investment and what it delivers in terms of outputs and outcomes; the consequences of each of the three reduction scenarios presented honestly — what services would be cut, which populations would be most affected, what outcomes would deteriorate, and what the medium-term fiscal consequences (demand for other services, reduced maternal employment) would be; a prioritisation analysis within the 10% reduction scenario identifying which elements of the programme should be protected and which could be reduced with least harm; any efficiency opportunities that could deliver savings without proportionate service reduction; and the minimum funding floor below which the programme cannot function effectively.","tags":["finance","spending-review","budget","policy"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-22T11:15:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-97","title":"Public Sector Ethics Investigation Procedure","prompt":"You are the Director General of a public sector integrity commission. Design a comprehensive investigation procedure for handling misconduct allegations against senior public officials (Grade 7 and above, including ministers). The procedure must be fair to the subject of the investigation while being robust enough to be credible to the public. Cover: the triage process for assessing incoming allegations — how to distinguish allegations that warrant formal investigation from vexatious complaints or matters better handled through line management; the decision criteria for opening a formal investigation; appointment and independence requirements for investigators; the investigation process including evidence gathering powers (document production, witness interviews, forensic analysis), the right of the subject to be informed of the allegations and respond to evidence, legal representation rights, and the treatment of third-party witnesses; the standard of proof to be applied; the range of outcomes available — from no case to answer through to dismissal, referral to prosecution, and public report; appeal rights and the appeals body; transparency and public reporting — when findings are published and in what form, balancing accountability with fairness to subjects; and the protections against the investigation process being used for political purposes.","tags":["ethics","integrity","investigation","governance"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-22T11:20:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-98","title":"National Statistics Quality Framework","prompt":"You are the Chief Statistician of a national statistical office. The government has committed to strengthening the quality, independence, and public trust of official statistics following a controversy in which ministers were found to have seen and commented on unemployment figures before public release, and separate criticism that the methodology for calculating inflation had not been updated to reflect modern consumption patterns. Design a National Statistics Quality Framework and governance reform. The framework should cover: the statistical independence framework — the legal and operational basis for the statistical office's independence from ministerial influence, including pre-release access restrictions and the independence of the National Statistician; the quality standards for official statistics — covering relevance, accuracy and reliability, timeliness, accessibility, coherence, and interpretability, drawn from UN Fundamental Principles and European Statistics Code of Practice; a methodology review process for bringing statistical methodologies up to date systematically; the peer review and external scrutiny programme; transparency requirements for statistical methods, data sources, and revisions policy; user engagement — how the statistical office ensures statistics serve the needs of policy makers, researchers, media, and citizens rather than just government; public communication standards to prevent misuse of statistics; and the statistics authority's oversight function.","tags":["statistics","data","integrity","government"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-22T11:25:00Z","featured":false},

    {"id":"gov-99","title":"Crisis Government Continuity Plan","prompt":"You are the Head of Civil Contingencies for a national government. Design a Government Continuity of Operations Plan (COOP) to ensure that critical government functions continue in the event of a severe, prolonged disruption — specifically a scenario where a major cyberattack has disabled the government's central IT infrastructure and three government buildings in the capital are inaccessible due to a public health emergency simultaneously. The plan must cover: the identification of Mission Critical Functions (MCFs) — the government functions that must continue within 24 hours, 72 hours, and 7 days of a disruptive event, including which functions can be temporarily suspended; the delegation of authority — the chain of succession for each major office of state if principal office holders are unavailable; alternate operating locations — the criteria for selecting alternate sites, the pre-positioned resources required, and the activation protocol; manual fallback processes for the 12 most critical IT-dependent functions; communications protocols for government-to-government, government-to-public, and government-to-parliament communication when normal channels are unavailable; inter-agency coordination with emergency services, critical infrastructure operators, and allied governments; the plan activation criteria and the decision-making authority; testing and exercise requirements; and the plan maintenance cycle.","tags":["emergency","continuity","crisis","security"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-22T11:30:00Z","featured":true},

    {"id":"gov-100","title":"Future of Government: 2040 Scenarios","prompt":"You are a strategic foresight adviser commissioned by a Cabinet Office to develop a scenarios report to help senior officials think about how the role and form of government could change by 2040. Write a structured scenarios report. First, identify the five most critical driving forces that will shape government over the next 15 years: the impact of artificial intelligence on public sector work; demographic ageing and its fiscal and service implications; the erosion of trust in government institutions; climate change requiring a major expansion of state activity; and geopolitical fragmentation reshaping international governance. Second, develop four distinct scenarios (not just optimistic versus pessimistic but genuinely different futures): Scenario 1 — The Platform State (government as infrastructure and regulator, most services delivered by accredited private and third-sector providers through a government digital platform); Scenario 2 — The Resilience State (government expands significantly to manage cascading climate and security crises, with more direct provision and economic management); Scenario 3 — The Fragmented State (populism and distrust lead to devolution and local variation, with much weaker central coordination); Scenario 4 — The Adaptive State (AI enables personalised, proactive services, government becomes smaller but more targeted). For each scenario: describe the world in 2040, the key implications for the structure and size of government, and two strategic moves the government should make now that would be valuable in that scenario. Conclude with five 'no regret' moves valid across all scenarios.","tags":["foresight","strategy","future","government"],"author":"AINOW","created":"2024-02-22T11:35:00Z","featured":true}

];
